WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.610 align:middle line:90%


00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:02.440 align:middle line:84%
The following
content is provided

00:00:02.440 --> 00:00:06.340 align:middle line:84%
by MIT OpenCourseWare under
a Creative Commons license.

00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:08.470 align:middle line:84%
Additional information
about our license

00:00:08.470 --> 00:00:10.870 align:middle line:84%
and MIT OpenCourseWare
in general

00:00:10.870 --> 00:00:12.730 align:middle line:90%
is available at ocw.mit.edu.

00:00:12.730 --> 00:00:17.110 align:middle line:90%


00:00:17.110 --> 00:00:18.220 align:middle line:90%
GEORGE CHURCH: OK.

00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:23.410 align:middle line:90%
Welcome to the second proteins.

00:00:23.410 --> 00:00:27.160 align:middle line:84%
Just as with the RNA analyses,
we started with, in that case,

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:28.930 align:middle line:84%
a brief discussion
of RNA structure,

00:00:28.930 --> 00:00:31.450 align:middle line:84%
and then moved on
to RNA quantitation.

00:00:31.450 --> 00:00:36.400 align:middle line:84%
This case, we spent a bit more
time on protein structure,

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:37.900 align:middle line:84%
and we'll spend a
little less time

00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:41.830 align:middle line:84%
on protein quantitation, which
will be the main topic today.

00:00:41.830 --> 00:00:45.282 align:middle line:84%
But partly because the RNA
and protein quantitation

00:00:45.282 --> 00:00:47.740 align:middle line:84%
have many themes in common, so
we've covered some of those.

00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:53.290 align:middle line:84%
And we'll talk about how one can
integrate protein quantitation

00:00:53.290 --> 00:00:59.110 align:middle line:84%
with RNA quantitation
and with metabolism.

00:00:59.110 --> 00:01:02.380 align:middle line:84%
So last time we
talked about mainly

00:01:02.380 --> 00:01:04.989 align:middle line:84%
with structure and
interaction of proteins

00:01:04.989 --> 00:01:07.210 align:middle line:84%
with small molecules at
the structural level.

00:01:07.210 --> 00:01:09.520 align:middle line:84%
And this time we'll
be building up

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:13.450 align:middle line:84%
towards the quantitating
proteins and their interactions

00:01:13.450 --> 00:01:17.440 align:middle line:84%
with other proteins
of small molecules

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:22.060 align:middle line:84%
so that we can address
that as the first step

00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:24.160 align:middle line:84%
towards the network
analysis that

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:27.590 align:middle line:84%
will be the subject of the last
three lectures of the course.

00:01:27.590 --> 00:01:31.015 align:middle line:84%
So we'll get a hint of that
at the end of today's talk.

00:01:31.015 --> 00:01:36.520 align:middle line:90%


00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:39.620 align:middle line:84%
So at the beginning
of the course,

00:01:39.620 --> 00:01:41.320 align:middle line:84%
I had one slide
talking about how

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:46.700 align:middle line:84%
purification was a revolution
in many different fields--

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:49.210 align:middle line:84%
one of them resulting in
recombinant DNA and the Genome

00:01:49.210 --> 00:01:50.002 align:middle line:90%
Project, and so on.

00:01:50.002 --> 00:01:52.043 align:middle line:84%
Today we're going to talk
more about purification

00:01:52.043 --> 00:01:54.280 align:middle line:84%
from the standpoint from
what does it tell us

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:55.870 align:middle line:84%
about the properties
of the molecule

00:01:55.870 --> 00:01:59.860 align:middle line:84%
itself as opposed to just
purifying for its own sake.

00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:03.550 align:middle line:84%
How can we go in and data mine
and get the maximum value out

00:02:03.550 --> 00:02:05.140 align:middle line:90%
of purification?

00:02:05.140 --> 00:02:08.710 align:middle line:84%
The things that we
expect from purification

00:02:08.710 --> 00:02:11.200 align:middle line:84%
is to reduce some
source of noise.

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:13.900 align:middle line:84%
That is to say, in the
process of identification

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:19.540 align:middle line:84%
or quantitation of proteins
or their components,

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:23.410 align:middle line:90%
we want to remove sources--

00:02:23.410 --> 00:02:27.040 align:middle line:84%
the major source of noise would
be environmental contamination,

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:31.000 align:middle line:84%
or more often contamination
with other bona fide members

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:34.330 align:middle line:84%
of a mixture, other proteins
that may be very abundant

00:02:34.330 --> 00:02:36.760 align:middle line:84%
or breakdown products
or related products.

00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:40.400 align:middle line:84%
And we want to separate it into
enough different components

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:42.790 align:middle line:84%
so that the major ones are
off in their own little bin

00:02:42.790 --> 00:02:45.040 align:middle line:90%
and don't interfere too much.

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:49.450 align:middle line:84%
So the second reason why
we might want to purify

00:02:49.450 --> 00:02:52.030 align:middle line:84%
is to prepare materials
for in vitro experiments.

00:02:52.030 --> 00:02:54.430 align:middle line:84%
While we're studying networks
in the next three classes,

00:02:54.430 --> 00:02:56.215 align:middle line:84%
we'll find that
the real problem is

00:02:56.215 --> 00:02:58.090 align:middle line:84%
that they're complicated
enough that you need

00:02:58.090 --> 00:03:03.940 align:middle line:84%
some way of isolating one
network component from all

00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:05.660 align:middle line:90%
the possible interactions.

00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:08.235 align:middle line:84%
And one way to do that is to
purify that network component,

00:03:08.235 --> 00:03:09.610 align:middle line:84%
or a number of
network components

00:03:09.610 --> 00:03:11.920 align:middle line:84%
out and make a subnetwork
completely artificially

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:12.770 align:middle line:90%
in vitro.

00:03:12.770 --> 00:03:15.010 align:middle line:84%
So that's another
use of purification.

00:03:15.010 --> 00:03:16.863 align:middle line:84%
And finally-- and
this is the main theme

00:03:16.863 --> 00:03:18.280 align:middle line:84%
for the next couple
of slides-- is

00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:19.930 align:middle line:84%
to discover
biochemical properties

00:03:19.930 --> 00:03:21.830 align:middle line:90%
about that component itself.

00:03:21.830 --> 00:03:25.090 align:middle line:84%
So what can you glean from
the purification process?

00:03:25.090 --> 00:03:27.970 align:middle line:84%
And this requires careful
use of purification.

00:03:27.970 --> 00:03:30.670 align:middle line:84%
Now, many of these
methods were developed

00:03:30.670 --> 00:03:33.460 align:middle line:84%
because they work well,
but now we go back

00:03:33.460 --> 00:03:35.020 align:middle line:84%
and we look at which
ones can give us

00:03:35.020 --> 00:03:37.510 align:middle line:84%
information about the
biology and the chemistry

00:03:37.510 --> 00:03:38.930 align:middle line:90%
of the system.

00:03:38.930 --> 00:03:43.840 align:middle line:84%
And so we have the charge
of a molecule, which

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:45.460 align:middle line:84%
we talked about
last time as being

00:03:45.460 --> 00:03:49.660 align:middle line:84%
a very important relatively
long range interaction

00:03:49.660 --> 00:03:51.620 align:middle line:90%
1/r interaction.

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:54.850 align:middle line:84%
And here are two methods
I mentioned, one involving

00:03:54.850 --> 00:03:59.350 align:middle line:84%
electric field, isoelectric
focusing, which determines

00:03:59.350 --> 00:04:01.360 align:middle line:84%
the pH at which the
charge is neutral

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:03.130 align:middle line:90%
and there's no net movement.

00:04:03.130 --> 00:04:04.930 align:middle line:84%
In ion exchange
chromatography, we

00:04:04.930 --> 00:04:07.330 align:middle line:84%
have a mobile phase
and a solid phase.

00:04:07.330 --> 00:04:11.290 align:middle line:84%
And charge enters
prominently into that.

00:04:11.290 --> 00:04:16.930 align:middle line:84%
Size is something that will be
a recurring theme tonight, where

00:04:16.930 --> 00:04:20.920 align:middle line:84%
we'll be talking about the
mass of protein complexes,

00:04:20.920 --> 00:04:23.510 align:middle line:84%
the mass of individual
protein subunits,

00:04:23.510 --> 00:04:27.817 align:middle line:84%
and the mass of peptides cleaved
out of those basic subunits.

00:04:27.817 --> 00:04:30.400 align:middle line:84%
And you can see there are quite
a number of different methods.

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:34.940 align:middle line:84%
Sedimentation velocity will
be one that we will use--

00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:38.950 align:middle line:90%
electrophoresis, and so on.

00:04:38.950 --> 00:04:41.350 align:middle line:84%
Solubility and
hydrophobicity I'll

00:04:41.350 --> 00:04:44.050 align:middle line:84%
lump together here
as properties,

00:04:44.050 --> 00:04:47.110 align:middle line:84%
kind of bulk properties of
the amino acid composition

00:04:47.110 --> 00:04:48.580 align:middle line:90%
of peptides and proteins.

00:04:48.580 --> 00:04:54.880 align:middle line:84%
And they refer to their affinity
for hydrophobic solid phases

00:04:54.880 --> 00:05:02.050 align:middle line:84%
or hydrophobic mobile
phases, solvents, and so on.

00:05:02.050 --> 00:05:05.560 align:middle line:84%
The biological significance
of hydrophobicity

00:05:05.560 --> 00:05:12.280 align:middle line:84%
might be the affinity
for lipid bilayers

00:05:12.280 --> 00:05:14.200 align:middle line:84%
or affinity for other
hydrophobic patches

00:05:14.200 --> 00:05:16.370 align:middle line:90%
of other proteins.

00:05:16.370 --> 00:05:19.150 align:middle line:84%
Now, we have-- the
size tells us something

00:05:19.150 --> 00:05:22.900 align:middle line:84%
about the stoichiometry of
protein-protein interactions

00:05:22.900 --> 00:05:28.540 align:middle line:84%
when we're looking at native
measures of size of complexes.

00:05:28.540 --> 00:05:31.450 align:middle line:84%
And other indications
of specific binding,

00:05:31.450 --> 00:05:35.650 align:middle line:84%
whether it's between proteins
or a protein in a small molecule

00:05:35.650 --> 00:05:37.960 align:middle line:84%
can be detected by
affinity chromatography

00:05:37.960 --> 00:05:44.890 align:middle line:84%
or another related method as
[INAUDIBLE] precipitation,

00:05:44.890 --> 00:05:48.700 align:middle line:84%
where you'll have one ligand,
like an antibody, which

00:05:48.700 --> 00:05:51.040 align:middle line:84%
is specific for a
particular protein epitope

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:53.110 align:middle line:84%
and will pull down
all the proteins that

00:05:53.110 --> 00:06:00.760 align:middle line:84%
are associated with that
epitope, that surface property.

00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:02.990 align:middle line:84%
And another
sedimentation method--

00:06:02.990 --> 00:06:05.410 align:middle line:84%
now, the first
sedimentation method

00:06:05.410 --> 00:06:07.900 align:middle line:84%
was a velocity method,
a kinetic method,

00:06:07.900 --> 00:06:12.520 align:middle line:84%
where the largest
particles, once you

00:06:12.520 --> 00:06:15.100 align:middle line:84%
take into account buoyant
density, all other things being

00:06:15.100 --> 00:06:18.460 align:middle line:84%
equal, the largest particles
will sediment most quickly.

00:06:18.460 --> 00:06:22.090 align:middle line:84%
If you set it up so that things
are nearly equally buoyant

00:06:22.090 --> 00:06:25.660 align:middle line:84%
and you build up a density
gradient by centrifugal field,

00:06:25.660 --> 00:06:28.360 align:middle line:84%
then you get
particles separating

00:06:28.360 --> 00:06:32.110 align:middle line:84%
by their properties,
which can include

00:06:32.110 --> 00:06:36.930 align:middle line:84%
the binding of metal
ions, which greatly affect

00:06:36.930 --> 00:06:39.210 align:middle line:84%
the density of nucleic
acids, for example,

00:06:39.210 --> 00:06:43.890 align:middle line:84%
and hence, nucleic
acid protein complexes.

00:06:43.890 --> 00:06:48.270 align:middle line:84%
Now, this is a
particularly awesome pair.

00:06:48.270 --> 00:06:52.290 align:middle line:84%
And historically, it figures
prominently into proteomics.

00:06:52.290 --> 00:06:56.550 align:middle line:90%
It's still quite viable.

00:06:56.550 --> 00:06:59.190 align:middle line:84%
And they illustrate
some important points.

00:06:59.190 --> 00:07:05.130 align:middle line:84%
When we talk about the
mass of a native protein

00:07:05.130 --> 00:07:08.730 align:middle line:84%
or of a protein that's been
denatured by a detergent

00:07:08.730 --> 00:07:14.220 align:middle line:84%
micelle, such as Sodium
Dodecyl Sulfate, SDS,

00:07:14.220 --> 00:07:19.410 align:middle line:84%
its mass can be resolved
by fairly potent

00:07:19.410 --> 00:07:21.210 align:middle line:84%
polyacrylamide gel
electrophoresis, where

00:07:21.210 --> 00:07:24.330 align:middle line:84%
you get sieving, where
the gel causes sieving.

00:07:24.330 --> 00:07:28.740 align:middle line:84%
And this micelle, this
detergent micelle size itself,

00:07:28.740 --> 00:07:32.010 align:middle line:84%
or the micelle is dependent
upon the size of the unfolded

00:07:32.010 --> 00:07:34.890 align:middle line:84%
or partially unfolded
protein chain.

00:07:34.890 --> 00:07:44.970 align:middle line:84%
And so you actually get a
fairly good calibratable plot

00:07:44.970 --> 00:07:48.990 align:middle line:84%
of mass of the protein
versus the mobility

00:07:48.990 --> 00:07:52.230 align:middle line:84%
in an electric field when
the protein embeds itself

00:07:52.230 --> 00:07:58.740 align:middle line:90%
in this detergent micelle.

00:07:58.740 --> 00:08:06.060 align:middle line:84%
So this observation of the
potentability of this detergent

00:08:06.060 --> 00:08:08.760 align:middle line:84%
to denature proteins,
and then to resolve based

00:08:08.760 --> 00:08:11.580 align:middle line:84%
on mass with the small
exceptions of very

00:08:11.580 --> 00:08:14.910 align:middle line:84%
hydrophobic proteins or very
carbohydrate-rich proteins--

00:08:14.910 --> 00:08:19.200 align:middle line:84%
most other proteins will
have a very nice calibratable

00:08:19.200 --> 00:08:20.520 align:middle line:90%
relationship.

00:08:20.520 --> 00:08:23.370 align:middle line:84%
Similarly, the
charge on a protein

00:08:23.370 --> 00:08:26.880 align:middle line:84%
approaches 0 as the
pH gets to the point

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:29.490 align:middle line:84%
where it's titrating out all
the type titratable groups.

00:08:29.490 --> 00:08:31.020 align:middle line:90%
And this can be calculated.

00:08:31.020 --> 00:08:34.659 align:middle line:84%
And the resolution of this is
about 1 part in 100 or better.

00:08:34.659 --> 00:08:38.190 align:middle line:84%
So both of these
together are two very

00:08:38.190 --> 00:08:39.270 align:middle line:90%
high resolution methods.

00:08:39.270 --> 00:08:41.280 align:middle line:84%
And you can think of
trying to divide-- you

00:08:41.280 --> 00:08:43.559 align:middle line:90%
have a complex protein mixture.

00:08:43.559 --> 00:08:46.110 align:middle line:84%
You want to divide it up into
a lot of separate bins, maybe

00:08:46.110 --> 00:08:47.400 align:middle line:90%
100 of each.

00:08:47.400 --> 00:08:49.360 align:middle line:84%
And if you combine
two dimensions,

00:08:49.360 --> 00:08:51.360 align:middle line:84%
as you often do
here, where you'll

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:56.280 align:middle line:84%
run first isoelectric, and
then second the SDS dimension,

00:08:56.280 --> 00:08:58.020 align:middle line:84%
now you got 100 bins
in one dimension,

00:08:58.020 --> 00:09:00.060 align:middle line:84%
and each of those bins
turns into 100 bins

00:09:00.060 --> 00:09:01.120 align:middle line:90%
in the second dimension.

00:09:01.120 --> 00:09:03.078 align:middle line:84%
So theoretically, you've
got on the order of 10

00:09:03.078 --> 00:09:04.080 align:middle line:90%
to the fourth bins.

00:09:04.080 --> 00:09:07.680 align:middle line:84%
And if some of the
things that you want to--

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:10.230 align:middle line:84%
some of the rare proteins
you want to analyze

00:09:10.230 --> 00:09:11.730 align:middle line:84%
are in one of those
bins, you might

00:09:11.730 --> 00:09:15.090 align:middle line:84%
have gotten as much as a 10
to the fourth-fold enrichment

00:09:15.090 --> 00:09:17.710 align:middle line:84%
for those rare proteins away
from the more common ones,

00:09:17.710 --> 00:09:21.630 align:middle line:84%
which are all too
easy to stumble upon.

00:09:21.630 --> 00:09:25.140 align:middle line:84%
Now, before we get
to an actual example

00:09:25.140 --> 00:09:29.640 align:middle line:84%
over two-dimensional gel,
I want to motivate this

00:09:29.640 --> 00:09:32.700 align:middle line:84%
by the computational
components to it

00:09:32.700 --> 00:09:35.400 align:middle line:84%
and the computational
biology that you

00:09:35.400 --> 00:09:39.870 align:middle line:84%
can get from these
multi-dimensional separations.

00:09:39.870 --> 00:09:44.010 align:middle line:84%
And this comes from
our recurring interest

00:09:44.010 --> 00:09:46.470 align:middle line:84%
in comparing whenever
we can calculate

00:09:46.470 --> 00:09:50.730 align:middle line:84%
a property of a system, or
in this case of a protein,

00:09:50.730 --> 00:09:54.100 align:middle line:84%
we do so, and we compare
it with the observations.

00:09:54.100 --> 00:09:59.430 align:middle line:84%
And some of the properties
here are the localization

00:09:59.430 --> 00:10:00.930 align:middle line:84%
in the cell, which
essentially is

00:10:00.930 --> 00:10:04.350 align:middle line:84%
an association of the
protein as it's made,

00:10:04.350 --> 00:10:06.930 align:middle line:84%
the post-synthetic
modifications to the proteins,

00:10:06.930 --> 00:10:09.330 align:middle line:84%
such as proteolysis
and phosphorylation,

00:10:09.330 --> 00:10:14.520 align:middle line:84%
and so on, its
charge, and its mass.

00:10:14.520 --> 00:10:18.840 align:middle line:84%
And here is shown a plot
of calculated charge,

00:10:18.840 --> 00:10:22.770 align:middle line:84%
or isoelectric
point in pH units.

00:10:22.770 --> 00:10:25.950 align:middle line:84%
P being the negative
logarithm of the hydrogen ion

00:10:25.950 --> 00:10:26.972 align:middle line:90%
concentration.

00:10:26.972 --> 00:10:28.680 align:middle line:84%
So it's calculated on
the horizontal axis

00:10:28.680 --> 00:10:30.670 align:middle line:84%
and observed on
the vertical axis.

00:10:30.670 --> 00:10:37.530 align:middle line:84%
And what we're seeing here is if
there were a perfect x equal y

00:10:37.530 --> 00:10:39.870 align:middle line:84%
relationship here, where
calculated and observed

00:10:39.870 --> 00:10:42.900 align:middle line:84%
were the same, then all the
dots would lie on this line,

00:10:42.900 --> 00:10:45.600 align:middle line:84%
and though the
outliers, initially they

00:10:45.600 --> 00:10:49.680 align:middle line:84%
were such things as frame
shifts of the DNA sequence,

00:10:49.680 --> 00:10:52.050 align:middle line:84%
producing a wrong
calculated protein.

00:10:52.050 --> 00:10:54.780 align:middle line:84%
Once those were
corrected, then there

00:10:54.780 --> 00:10:57.120 align:middle line:84%
were observed proteolytic
cleavages, which

00:10:57.120 --> 00:10:59.460 align:middle line:84%
could be mapped down through
the exact amino acid,

00:10:59.460 --> 00:11:02.430 align:middle line:84%
and then those corrected
a few more on to the line.

00:11:02.430 --> 00:11:05.250 align:middle line:84%
And the remainders were other
post-synthetic modifications,

00:11:05.250 --> 00:11:07.210 align:middle line:90%
such as phosphorylation.

00:11:07.210 --> 00:11:10.200 align:middle line:84%
So here's a reason to
embrace your outliers.

00:11:10.200 --> 00:11:12.930 align:middle line:84%
Each one of these things
is an exciting story,

00:11:12.930 --> 00:11:17.340 align:middle line:84%
where you either have a
correction to a previous data

00:11:17.340 --> 00:11:24.450 align:middle line:84%
type or a new discovery of a
post-synthetic modification.

00:11:24.450 --> 00:11:29.220 align:middle line:84%
Now, how do we actually
calculate all these facts

00:11:29.220 --> 00:11:31.590 align:middle line:90%
about protein properties?

00:11:31.590 --> 00:11:34.230 align:middle line:84%
Some of these have
biological significance,

00:11:34.230 --> 00:11:35.940 align:middle line:90%
which we've listed.

00:11:35.940 --> 00:11:37.710 align:middle line:84%
Where it is in the
cell, obviously,

00:11:37.710 --> 00:11:40.380 align:middle line:84%
matters to its carrying
out its function.

00:11:40.380 --> 00:11:43.030 align:middle line:84%
How big it is determines what
other proteins are associated,

00:11:43.030 --> 00:11:43.630 align:middle line:90%
and so on.

00:11:43.630 --> 00:11:44.820 align:middle line:90%
So how do we calculate this?

00:11:44.820 --> 00:11:47.370 align:middle line:84%
The protein charge which
was in the previous slide

00:11:47.370 --> 00:11:50.040 align:middle line:84%
is a simple linear
function where

00:11:50.040 --> 00:11:53.680 align:middle line:84%
you sum up the pKas of each
of the individual amino acids.

00:11:53.680 --> 00:11:56.280 align:middle line:84%
Now, pKa, again, p means
negative logarithm,

00:11:56.280 --> 00:11:59.820 align:middle line:84%
and the Ka means the
equilibrium association

00:11:59.820 --> 00:12:07.500 align:middle line:84%
constant of the proton with each
of these chargeable residues.

00:12:07.500 --> 00:12:11.370 align:middle line:84%
So depending on the pH,
there are a wide variety

00:12:11.370 --> 00:12:13.350 align:middle line:84%
of nearly
physiological pHs where

00:12:13.350 --> 00:12:15.950 align:middle line:84%
[INAUDIBLE] and histidines
will be positively charged,

00:12:15.950 --> 00:12:21.210 align:middle line:84%
the blues, and the red ones
can be tyrosine and cysteine.

00:12:21.210 --> 00:12:24.630 align:middle line:84%
Especially aspartate
and glutamate

00:12:24.630 --> 00:12:28.050 align:middle line:84%
can be negatively charged
in the range of pHs

00:12:28.050 --> 00:12:30.790 align:middle line:84%
that we saw in the
previous slide.

00:12:30.790 --> 00:12:34.170 align:middle line:84%
And so this is calculated
as that simple sum.

00:12:34.170 --> 00:12:42.810 align:middle line:84%
And protein mass is
calibratable with knowns.

00:12:42.810 --> 00:12:45.720 align:middle line:84%
Even if you have a very
complex empirical relationship,

00:12:45.720 --> 00:12:49.620 align:middle line:84%
such as that detergent binding
FBS gel electrophoresis.

00:12:49.620 --> 00:12:52.440 align:middle line:84%
It sounds very-- too
many moving parts

00:12:52.440 --> 00:12:53.760 align:middle line:90%
to be completely theoretical.

00:12:53.760 --> 00:12:57.750 align:middle line:84%
But if you calibrate it with
good known proteins, or protein

00:12:57.750 --> 00:13:01.050 align:middle line:84%
complexes if you're doing
a native electrophoresis

00:13:01.050 --> 00:13:05.190 align:middle line:84%
or native
sedimentation velocity,

00:13:05.190 --> 00:13:08.190 align:middle line:84%
then you can get a curve where
you can interpolate and find

00:13:08.190 --> 00:13:11.565 align:middle line:84%
masses quite accurately, or
at least around about 2%.

00:13:11.565 --> 00:13:14.340 align:middle line:90%


00:13:14.340 --> 00:13:16.860 align:middle line:84%
But mass spectrometry
is commonly

00:13:16.860 --> 00:13:20.160 align:middle line:84%
applied to peptide masses,
sometimes whole protein masses.

00:13:20.160 --> 00:13:23.250 align:middle line:84%
And here, assuming
the mass spectrometer

00:13:23.250 --> 00:13:25.350 align:middle line:84%
is properly calibrated
and so forth, this

00:13:25.350 --> 00:13:26.940 align:middle line:90%
is a simple isotope sum.

00:13:26.940 --> 00:13:31.050 align:middle line:84%
And this can be carried out
sometimes to four or six

00:13:31.050 --> 00:13:33.780 align:middle line:90%
significant figures.

00:13:33.780 --> 00:13:41.550 align:middle line:84%
And it really is a simple
sum of the isotopes measured

00:13:41.550 --> 00:13:42.390 align:middle line:90%
by physics.

00:13:42.390 --> 00:13:44.802 align:middle line:84%
And this can include
post-synthetic modifications.

00:13:44.802 --> 00:13:46.260 align:middle line:84%
As you're getting
down to peptides,

00:13:46.260 --> 00:13:48.570 align:middle line:84%
the post-synthetic modification
becomes a much larger

00:13:48.570 --> 00:13:53.745 align:middle line:84%
fractional effect on the
measures that you're making.

00:13:53.745 --> 00:13:56.670 align:middle line:90%


00:13:56.670 --> 00:13:58.800 align:middle line:84%
Not only the mass, but
the liquid chromatography

00:13:58.800 --> 00:14:02.730 align:middle line:84%
properties of either a protein
or a peptide can be calculated.

00:14:02.730 --> 00:14:04.930 align:middle line:84%
Here you take amino
acid composition

00:14:04.930 --> 00:14:07.980 align:middle line:84%
and do linear regression
on a calibration set.

00:14:07.980 --> 00:14:14.010 align:middle line:84%
And you can get precision on
the order of 5% or better.

00:14:14.010 --> 00:14:17.460 align:middle line:84%
A subset of localization,
we have motifs.

00:14:17.460 --> 00:14:19.380 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes
hydrophobicity is a part

00:14:19.380 --> 00:14:22.530 align:middle line:84%
of those motifs in
their description.

00:14:22.530 --> 00:14:24.660 align:middle line:84%
Expression would
not be something--

00:14:24.660 --> 00:14:28.050 align:middle line:84%
we've seen the motifs that
are involved in regulation

00:14:28.050 --> 00:14:29.710 align:middle line:90%
of transcription and so forth.

00:14:29.710 --> 00:14:33.660 align:middle line:84%
But kind of the shortcut that
might take you directly there

00:14:33.660 --> 00:14:36.910 align:middle line:84%
in certain cases is the
codon adaptation index.

00:14:36.910 --> 00:14:40.350 align:middle line:84%
This is something
where the hypothesis is

00:14:40.350 --> 00:14:43.470 align:middle line:84%
that you can go directly
from the nucleotide sequence

00:14:43.470 --> 00:14:45.780 align:middle line:84%
of a protein coding
region, and if it

00:14:45.780 --> 00:14:49.080 align:middle line:84%
uses codons that
are very abundant,

00:14:49.080 --> 00:14:51.630 align:middle line:84%
that correspond to very
abundant transfer RNAs,

00:14:51.630 --> 00:14:52.890 align:middle line:90%
then that protein--

00:14:52.890 --> 00:14:55.830 align:middle line:84%
then that's saying that
the evolutionary pressures

00:14:55.830 --> 00:14:59.310 align:middle line:84%
producing that particular
choice of codons

00:14:59.310 --> 00:15:02.520 align:middle line:84%
is revealing that that protein
is going to be high abundance.

00:15:02.520 --> 00:15:06.930 align:middle line:84%
So in a way, this is a way
of going directly without--

00:15:06.930 --> 00:15:15.060 align:middle line:84%
due to this observation and
somewhat logical expectation.

00:15:15.060 --> 00:15:15.780 align:middle line:90%
so

00:15:15.780 --> 00:15:19.080 align:middle line:84%
Now we have all sorts
of separation methods

00:15:19.080 --> 00:15:21.330 align:middle line:84%
and motivation for studying
them, more than just

00:15:21.330 --> 00:15:22.350 align:middle line:90%
using them.

00:15:22.350 --> 00:15:26.040 align:middle line:84%
But now we want to look at a
particular case of separation.

00:15:26.040 --> 00:15:30.300 align:middle line:84%
We're talking about complexes
and protein localization.

00:15:30.300 --> 00:15:42.120 align:middle line:84%
So this here is another example
of a two-dimensional gel, where

00:15:42.120 --> 00:15:49.110 align:middle line:84%
the isoelectric point
is on the x-axis here

00:15:49.110 --> 00:15:53.070 align:middle line:84%
and the vertical axis is this
estimate of molecular mass,

00:15:53.070 --> 00:15:56.160 align:middle line:84%
provided by the association
of the protein with the SDS

00:15:56.160 --> 00:15:59.940 align:middle line:84%
micelles and the effect
on gel electrophoresis.

00:15:59.940 --> 00:16:04.890 align:middle line:84%
And this is a 2D gel, a small
section of it, not the full pH

00:16:04.890 --> 00:16:06.900 align:middle line:84%
range nor the full
molecular weight range,

00:16:06.900 --> 00:16:13.210 align:middle line:84%
but this has a good fraction of
the proteins that are secreted.

00:16:13.210 --> 00:16:15.720 align:middle line:84%
Now, to what extent
can we calculate this?

00:16:15.720 --> 00:16:18.190 align:middle line:84%
We've shown a number of
calculations and observations

00:16:18.190 --> 00:16:18.900 align:middle line:90%
so far.

00:16:18.900 --> 00:16:24.600 align:middle line:84%
And we pointed out in last
class that to some extent

00:16:24.600 --> 00:16:27.730 align:middle line:84%
the transmembrane regions
of a protein could--

00:16:27.730 --> 00:16:31.440 align:middle line:84%
this is one of the better
algorithms in protein sequence

00:16:31.440 --> 00:16:32.600 align:middle line:90%
gazing.

00:16:32.600 --> 00:16:34.890 align:middle line:84%
And taking that
one step further,

00:16:34.890 --> 00:16:36.800 align:middle line:84%
you can actually
say, OK, we know

00:16:36.800 --> 00:16:41.330 align:middle line:84%
that this might have a
motif or two that interact

00:16:41.330 --> 00:16:44.840 align:middle line:84%
with membranes, or
somehow are part

00:16:44.840 --> 00:16:49.010 align:middle line:84%
of the process by which
proteins are targeted

00:16:49.010 --> 00:16:52.130 align:middle line:84%
and move across
membranes so that you

00:16:52.130 --> 00:16:54.830 align:middle line:84%
can divide it into several
different subcellular

00:16:54.830 --> 00:16:56.360 align:middle line:90%
localizations--

00:16:56.360 --> 00:16:58.670 align:middle line:84%
in eukaryotes, the
mitochondrial localization,

00:16:58.670 --> 00:17:03.293 align:middle line:84%
and chloroplasts
in plants, secreted

00:17:03.293 --> 00:17:05.210 align:middle line:84%
proteins that go all the
way across the plasma

00:17:05.210 --> 00:17:08.819 align:middle line:84%
membrane, and other locations,
such as within the membrane.

00:17:08.819 --> 00:17:13.220 align:middle line:84%
And this can have
an 85% success rate,

00:17:13.220 --> 00:17:15.630 align:middle line:90%
meaning a 15% false negative.

00:17:15.630 --> 00:17:18.770 align:middle line:84%
And you can see modest
false positives here,

00:17:18.770 --> 00:17:25.150 align:middle line:84%
too, over predictions
in 295 transmembranes.

00:17:25.150 --> 00:17:28.700 align:middle line:90%


00:17:28.700 --> 00:17:31.900 align:middle line:84%
So let's return to
mass, and this time

00:17:31.900 --> 00:17:33.550 align:middle line:84%
in the context of
mass spectrometry.

00:17:33.550 --> 00:17:34.510 align:middle line:90%
What do we have--

00:17:34.510 --> 00:17:39.510 align:middle line:90%


00:17:39.510 --> 00:17:40.710 align:middle line:90%
what's our starting point?

00:17:40.710 --> 00:17:43.310 align:middle line:84%
Well, if you look on the
far left side of slide 11,

00:17:43.310 --> 00:17:47.660 align:middle line:84%
you'll see the simplest
of the atoms, hydrogen,

00:17:47.660 --> 00:17:51.350 align:middle line:84%
and you would expect this
to have an atomic mass of 1.

00:17:51.350 --> 00:17:56.940 align:middle line:84%
Well, since carbon-12 is
assumed to be precisely 12,

00:17:56.940 --> 00:17:59.930 align:middle line:84%
it turns out that hydrogen
is not precisely 1 when

00:17:59.930 --> 00:18:01.760 align:middle line:90%
you actually measure it.

00:18:01.760 --> 00:18:05.990 align:middle line:84%
And it's not even good enough
for government standards.

00:18:05.990 --> 00:18:12.270 align:middle line:84%
When you actually, say, add
a CH2, it adds up to 14.

00:18:12.270 --> 00:18:14.480 align:middle line:84%
And that is
discriminatable from a

00:18:14.480 --> 00:18:18.110 align:middle line:84%
nitrogen-14 with a good
enough mass spectrometer.

00:18:18.110 --> 00:18:22.640 align:middle line:84%
As it turns out, for
most biochemical--

00:18:22.640 --> 00:18:25.280 align:middle line:84%
protein analyses,
you don't depend

00:18:25.280 --> 00:18:29.810 align:middle line:90%
on the sixth decimal point.

00:18:29.810 --> 00:18:32.930 align:middle line:90%
You can get away, really, with--

00:18:32.930 --> 00:18:35.930 align:middle line:84%
or certainly don't
depend on having

00:18:35.930 --> 00:18:40.280 align:middle line:84%
the 10 to the minus three atomic
mass units as your resolution.

00:18:40.280 --> 00:18:44.540 align:middle line:84%
But you do depend upon in
a much-- in a kilodalton

00:18:44.540 --> 00:18:49.970 align:middle line:84%
size peptide being able
to get 1 part in 10

00:18:49.970 --> 00:18:53.420 align:middle line:84%
to the fourth, 1
atomic mass unit.

00:18:53.420 --> 00:18:55.490 align:middle line:84%
Another big
consideration here is

00:18:55.490 --> 00:18:58.460 align:middle line:84%
that not only are these
things not exactly integers,

00:18:58.460 --> 00:19:01.160 align:middle line:84%
but in natural
abundance, they're

00:19:01.160 --> 00:19:04.910 align:middle line:84%
a mixture of the major
isotope on the far left

00:19:04.910 --> 00:19:07.700 align:middle line:84%
and the second and
sometimes third

00:19:07.700 --> 00:19:11.180 align:middle line:84%
and fourth stable isotopes,
usually non-radioactive

00:19:11.180 --> 00:19:14.360 align:middle line:84%
isotopes, which are
present in nature.

00:19:14.360 --> 00:19:19.310 align:middle line:84%
And the most abundant in
this particular list is C13.

00:19:19.310 --> 00:19:21.620 align:middle line:84%
And it's most abundant
in two senses.

00:19:21.620 --> 00:19:24.890 align:middle line:84%
One is it's the highest
fractional abundance

00:19:24.890 --> 00:19:28.160 align:middle line:90%
of any of these elements.

00:19:28.160 --> 00:19:32.270 align:middle line:84%
And secondly, carbon itself
is very common in peptides.

00:19:32.270 --> 00:19:34.820 align:middle line:84%
If you cleave your
protein up with trypsin,

00:19:34.820 --> 00:19:38.960 align:middle line:84%
which cleaves c-terminal
to lysines and arginines,

00:19:38.960 --> 00:19:43.130 align:middle line:84%
you'll get on the
order of 10 or 20

00:19:43.130 --> 00:19:45.410 align:middle line:90%
of these peptides per protein.

00:19:45.410 --> 00:19:47.420 align:middle line:84%
And they might be
10 amino acids long.

00:19:47.420 --> 00:19:51.770 align:middle line:84%
And so they might have on the
order of 40 carbons in them.

00:19:51.770 --> 00:19:56.150 align:middle line:84%
So now the fractional abundance
of C13 with 40 carbons

00:19:56.150 --> 00:19:57.965 align:middle line:90%
is getting close to unity.

00:19:57.965 --> 00:20:01.460 align:middle line:90%


00:20:01.460 --> 00:20:04.400 align:middle line:84%
And we'll see an example of
exactly how this plays out

00:20:04.400 --> 00:20:08.240 align:middle line:84%
in terms of the multiple
combinations of isotopes

00:20:08.240 --> 00:20:09.950 align:middle line:90%
that can occur.

00:20:09.950 --> 00:20:12.500 align:middle line:84%
Sulfur, on the other hand,
has more stable isotopes.

00:20:12.500 --> 00:20:14.510 align:middle line:84%
It has four different
stable isotopes,

00:20:14.510 --> 00:20:17.030 align:middle line:84%
but each one is a
fairly small fraction.

00:20:17.030 --> 00:20:19.820 align:middle line:84%
And the probability of having
a sulfur in a given peptide

00:20:19.820 --> 00:20:20.593 align:middle line:90%
is low.

00:20:20.593 --> 00:20:22.760 align:middle line:84%
I mean, that's the probability
of having one sulfur.

00:20:22.760 --> 00:20:26.150 align:middle line:84%
The probability of having 40
sulfurs is vanishingly small.

00:20:26.150 --> 00:20:28.340 align:middle line:84%
So we've gone
through calculating

00:20:28.340 --> 00:20:31.250 align:middle line:90%
all this charged mass.

00:20:31.250 --> 00:20:33.680 align:middle line:84%
Now we're going to do
liquid chromatography,

00:20:33.680 --> 00:20:38.030 align:middle line:84%
in particular
hydrophobic measures.

00:20:38.030 --> 00:20:40.280 align:middle line:84%
And this all falls
under the heading

00:20:40.280 --> 00:20:44.120 align:middle line:84%
of high-performance liquid
chromatography, which

00:20:44.120 --> 00:20:47.240 align:middle line:84%
is achieved under high
pressure typically

00:20:47.240 --> 00:20:50.810 align:middle line:90%
to get it to go rapidly.

00:20:50.810 --> 00:20:52.850 align:middle line:84%
And so you'll have
these little--

00:20:52.850 --> 00:20:54.770 align:middle line:84%
you'll digest your
protein with trypsin.

00:20:54.770 --> 00:20:56.330 align:middle line:84%
You get a series
of these peptides,

00:20:56.330 --> 00:21:01.250 align:middle line:90%
say, 10 amino acids or so.

00:21:01.250 --> 00:21:04.070 align:middle line:84%
And then they're injected
in a liquid phase.

00:21:04.070 --> 00:21:08.420 align:middle line:84%
They bind to the solid phase
by the hydrophobic properties.

00:21:08.420 --> 00:21:10.360 align:middle line:90%
You might have it.

00:21:10.360 --> 00:21:13.640 align:middle line:84%
And then you'll have a readout,
where, as a function of time,

00:21:13.640 --> 00:21:16.700 align:middle line:84%
you get abundance where the
peaks can be measured by mass

00:21:16.700 --> 00:21:20.160 align:middle line:90%
or ion counting and so on.

00:21:20.160 --> 00:21:22.760 align:middle line:84%
And these can be
collected or simply run

00:21:22.760 --> 00:21:25.513 align:middle line:90%
into a mass spectrometer.

00:21:25.513 --> 00:21:26.930 align:middle line:84%
There are going
to be two phases--

00:21:26.930 --> 00:21:28.760 align:middle line:84%
the mobile phase
and solid phase.

00:21:28.760 --> 00:21:35.000 align:middle line:84%
Talking about the
mobile phase first,

00:21:35.000 --> 00:21:39.200 align:middle line:84%
the hydrophobic tendency
of any given peptide

00:21:39.200 --> 00:21:41.210 align:middle line:84%
is going to be some
kind of related

00:21:41.210 --> 00:21:45.230 align:middle line:84%
to the sum of the individual
amino acid components.

00:21:45.230 --> 00:21:47.330 align:middle line:84%
And you can either have
a isocratic elution,

00:21:47.330 --> 00:21:50.300 align:middle line:84%
where you have basically
constant migration speed,

00:21:50.300 --> 00:21:54.290 align:middle line:84%
no change in the content
of the mobile phase,

00:21:54.290 --> 00:21:58.130 align:middle line:84%
or you can have the mobile
phase change its composition,

00:21:58.130 --> 00:22:01.340 align:middle line:84%
say, for something that's almost
entirely water at the beginning

00:22:01.340 --> 00:22:06.380 align:middle line:84%
to something that has a high
organic content, up to 40%

00:22:06.380 --> 00:22:11.450 align:middle line:84%
or so acetonitrile, and so that
at the end of the gradient,

00:22:11.450 --> 00:22:13.410 align:middle line:84%
even the most
hydrophobic peptides

00:22:13.410 --> 00:22:15.740 align:middle line:84%
now have just as much
affinity for the mobile phase

00:22:15.740 --> 00:22:18.785 align:middle line:84%
as the solid phase, and
they come flowing off.

00:22:18.785 --> 00:22:22.380 align:middle line:84%
The solid phase has a
number of different options.

00:22:22.380 --> 00:22:25.370 align:middle line:84%
The main one we've been talking
about, implicitly at least,

00:22:25.370 --> 00:22:28.490 align:middle line:84%
is the reverse
phase, where you have

00:22:28.490 --> 00:22:34.520 align:middle line:84%
the hydrophobic carbon-18
alkyl chains immobilized

00:22:34.520 --> 00:22:41.880 align:middle line:84%
to a highly porous
media in a column that

00:22:41.880 --> 00:22:44.850 align:middle line:90%
can withstand high pressure.

00:22:44.850 --> 00:22:49.980 align:middle line:84%
Or it can have differing
polarities, size exclusion,

00:22:49.980 --> 00:22:53.760 align:middle line:84%
the same sort of things
that we were talking

00:22:53.760 --> 00:22:57.165 align:middle line:84%
about with electrophoresis,
except now the force is

00:22:57.165 --> 00:22:59.640 align:middle line:84%
a non-electric field,
but it's simply

00:22:59.640 --> 00:23:02.940 align:middle line:84%
the pressure differentiable
between the injection

00:23:02.940 --> 00:23:03.960 align:middle line:90%
port and the output.

00:23:03.960 --> 00:23:08.330 align:middle line:90%


00:23:08.330 --> 00:23:11.390 align:middle line:84%
So here is a specific
case worked out

00:23:11.390 --> 00:23:16.670 align:middle line:84%
example of how you calculate
the affinity of a peptide

00:23:16.670 --> 00:23:20.210 align:middle line:90%
to a hydrophobic column.

00:23:20.210 --> 00:23:22.430 align:middle line:84%
This is a C18 column,
referring to the number

00:23:22.430 --> 00:23:24.350 align:middle line:90%
of carbons in the alkyl chain.

00:23:24.350 --> 00:23:27.748 align:middle line:84%
So you can see that this is like
a kind of a lipid type phase.

00:23:27.748 --> 00:23:30.290 align:middle line:84%
It's the sort of thing you might
see in the middle of a lipid

00:23:30.290 --> 00:23:31.820 align:middle line:90%
bilayer membrane.

00:23:31.820 --> 00:23:37.310 align:middle line:84%
And in this plot, you have
relative retention time

00:23:37.310 --> 00:23:39.260 align:middle line:90%
along the vertical axis.

00:23:39.260 --> 00:23:45.080 align:middle line:84%
And the residue number refers
to having short peptides

00:23:45.080 --> 00:23:48.620 align:middle line:84%
cleaved, sort of walking
along the protein.

00:23:48.620 --> 00:23:51.770 align:middle line:84%
These have been synthesized so
they march along the proteins,

00:23:51.770 --> 00:23:56.660 align:middle line:84%
very analogous to how
in an earlier class

00:23:56.660 --> 00:24:00.350 align:middle line:84%
Rosetta made synthetic
oligonucleotides marching along

00:24:00.350 --> 00:24:02.030 align:middle line:90%
the human genome.

00:24:02.030 --> 00:24:04.520 align:middle line:84%
And this was done in
order to calibrate

00:24:04.520 --> 00:24:07.640 align:middle line:84%
to see how the composition,
the amino acid composition

00:24:07.640 --> 00:24:10.490 align:middle line:84%
of a peptide might
affect its mobility

00:24:10.490 --> 00:24:12.060 align:middle line:90%
and allow you to calculate it.

00:24:12.060 --> 00:24:19.940 align:middle line:84%
And so what you end up with
here is a somewhat intuitive way

00:24:19.940 --> 00:24:23.400 align:middle line:84%
of summing the contributions
of each of these amino acids.

00:24:23.400 --> 00:24:28.760 align:middle line:84%
Now, remember, this is done
under fairly acidic conditions.

00:24:28.760 --> 00:24:32.210 align:middle line:84%
So the normally
charged acid groups

00:24:32.210 --> 00:24:34.770 align:middle line:84%
will now be protonated
and be neutral.

00:24:34.770 --> 00:24:38.930 align:middle line:84%
And so what we'll find is
mainly a spectrum from the very

00:24:38.930 --> 00:24:40.350 align:middle line:90%
slowest to be eluted.

00:24:40.350 --> 00:24:42.950 align:middle line:84%
They require the most
organic, and hence the most--

00:24:42.950 --> 00:24:46.400 align:middle line:84%
the longest retention time
would be the highly hydrophobic

00:24:46.400 --> 00:24:49.010 align:middle line:84%
aromatics, tryptophan
and phenylalanine,

00:24:49.010 --> 00:24:53.477 align:middle line:84%
have a component which you
obtain by linear regression

00:24:53.477 --> 00:24:54.560 align:middle line:90%
of each of these peptides.

00:24:54.560 --> 00:24:56.227 align:middle line:84%
You know the sequence
of these peptides.

00:24:56.227 --> 00:24:58.910 align:middle line:84%
You calculate their
composition vector.

00:24:58.910 --> 00:25:01.070 align:middle line:84%
And then you relate it
to the retention time

00:25:01.070 --> 00:25:03.800 align:middle line:84%
that's observed, so that the
lower plot is the observed

00:25:03.800 --> 00:25:04.430 align:middle line:90%
ones.

00:25:04.430 --> 00:25:05.990 align:middle line:84%
And then after you
do the regression,

00:25:05.990 --> 00:25:07.490 align:middle line:84%
you get a set of
these coefficients.

00:25:07.490 --> 00:25:09.950 align:middle line:84%
You can now plug them
in an additive sense

00:25:09.950 --> 00:25:11.677 align:middle line:90%
to make this calculated plot.

00:25:11.677 --> 00:25:13.760 align:middle line:84%
And you can see that there's
very good correlation

00:25:13.760 --> 00:25:15.477 align:middle line:90%
up and down.

00:25:15.477 --> 00:25:17.810 align:middle line:84%
It might have been better if
these authors had done this

00:25:17.810 --> 00:25:21.080 align:middle line:84%
as a plot of calculated
versus observed,

00:25:21.080 --> 00:25:23.600 align:middle line:84%
as we did in the previous one,
and then showed the scatter

00:25:23.600 --> 00:25:25.250 align:middle line:90%
and did a regression curve.

00:25:25.250 --> 00:25:26.580 align:middle line:90%
But you get the idea.

00:25:26.580 --> 00:25:29.600 align:middle line:84%
And so the most hydrophobic
ones are at the top,

00:25:29.600 --> 00:25:31.700 align:middle line:84%
and the ones that
are acid conditions

00:25:31.700 --> 00:25:32.870 align:middle line:90%
are the most highly charged.

00:25:32.870 --> 00:25:34.520 align:middle line:84%
There's the positive
charge ones, the lysine,

00:25:34.520 --> 00:25:36.062 align:middle line:84%
the histidine, and
arginine down here

00:25:36.062 --> 00:25:37.970 align:middle line:84%
at the bottom, and
the acidic ones,

00:25:37.970 --> 00:25:42.410 align:middle line:84%
since they're close to
neutral or near 0 here.

00:25:42.410 --> 00:25:45.980 align:middle line:84%
And there's a slight effect as
to whether the amino acid is

00:25:45.980 --> 00:25:47.240 align:middle line:90%
at the N-terminus or not.

00:25:47.240 --> 00:25:51.680 align:middle line:90%


00:25:51.680 --> 00:25:55.850 align:middle line:84%
So now we've calculated the
reverse phase behavior here

00:25:55.850 --> 00:26:00.260 align:middle line:84%
on the far left-hand
side of slide 17.

00:26:00.260 --> 00:26:02.330 align:middle line:84%
And this is separated
by hydrophobicity,

00:26:02.330 --> 00:26:05.450 align:middle line:84%
and then we have mass,
which we've outlined

00:26:05.450 --> 00:26:07.280 align:middle line:90%
how you can calculate the mass.

00:26:07.280 --> 00:26:09.890 align:middle line:84%
So now you can calculate
both its hydrophobicity

00:26:09.890 --> 00:26:12.350 align:middle line:84%
on the vertical axis, and
mass on the horizontal.

00:26:12.350 --> 00:26:15.170 align:middle line:84%
And you can make
this, in the computer,

00:26:15.170 --> 00:26:18.920 align:middle line:90%
this two-dimensional plot.

00:26:18.920 --> 00:26:21.060 align:middle line:84%
In this case, this is
actually observed data.

00:26:21.060 --> 00:26:24.500 align:middle line:84%
But you could also superimpose
on it the calculated ones.

00:26:24.500 --> 00:26:30.200 align:middle line:84%
These are slightly streakier
in the reverse phase retention

00:26:30.200 --> 00:26:34.820 align:middle line:84%
time, rt, just because of
the scale that one uses,

00:26:34.820 --> 00:26:39.235 align:middle line:84%
or the properties of
the separation method.

00:26:39.235 --> 00:26:40.610 align:middle line:84%
Now, if you blow
up-- if you take

00:26:40.610 --> 00:26:43.220 align:middle line:84%
one of these little
two-dimensional spots

00:26:43.220 --> 00:26:47.810 align:middle line:84%
and zoom in on it, and look
at it in greater detail,

00:26:47.810 --> 00:26:52.040 align:middle line:84%
you see that it actually
is a complex set of peaks

00:26:52.040 --> 00:26:56.030 align:middle line:84%
in the mass direction,
and fairly simple

00:26:56.030 --> 00:26:57.188 align:middle line:90%
in the retention time.

00:26:57.188 --> 00:26:58.730 align:middle line:84%
Now, you could say,
well, maybe these

00:26:58.730 --> 00:27:00.410 align:middle line:90%
are all different peptides.

00:27:00.410 --> 00:27:02.960 align:middle line:84%
But in fact-- and they
are, in a certain sense.

00:27:02.960 --> 00:27:07.640 align:middle line:84%
But they're trivial relatives of
this, where you need to think--

00:27:07.640 --> 00:27:11.370 align:middle line:84%
where you can data mine and
get additional information.

00:27:11.370 --> 00:27:18.740 align:middle line:84%
So each of these are separate
isotope peaks, meaning,

00:27:18.740 --> 00:27:21.680 align:middle line:84%
remember, this might have
40 carbons in this peptide.

00:27:21.680 --> 00:27:24.860 align:middle line:84%
And so it's going to be
a binomial distribution,

00:27:24.860 --> 00:27:30.410 align:middle line:84%
where this is the case where
you have zero carbon-13's.

00:27:30.410 --> 00:27:32.270 align:middle line:84%
In other words, it's
all carbon-12's.

00:27:32.270 --> 00:27:35.570 align:middle line:84%
And then the next
peak over to the right

00:27:35.570 --> 00:27:37.250 align:middle line:90%
is going to have one carbon-13.

00:27:37.250 --> 00:27:39.860 align:middle line:84%
The next peak over is going
to have two carbon-13's.

00:27:39.860 --> 00:27:43.070 align:middle line:84%
And n minus 2, where n is
the total number of carbons

00:27:43.070 --> 00:27:44.930 align:middle line:84%
in that peptide, which
would be carbon-12,

00:27:44.930 --> 00:27:46.580 align:middle line:84%
the most abundant
one, and so on.

00:27:46.580 --> 00:27:48.620 align:middle line:84%
And you'd get every
possible combination

00:27:48.620 --> 00:27:52.090 align:middle line:84%
in a binomial distribution
just as you would expect.

00:27:52.090 --> 00:27:53.090 align:middle line:90%
Now, what does this say?

00:27:53.090 --> 00:27:54.465 align:middle line:84%
It tells you at
least two things.

00:27:54.465 --> 00:27:57.800 align:middle line:84%
One is the distance between
these, you can see here,

00:27:57.800 --> 00:28:00.350 align:middle line:90%
is a half an atomic mass unit.

00:28:00.350 --> 00:28:02.630 align:middle line:84%
Well, how do we get a half
of an atomic mass unit?

00:28:02.630 --> 00:28:04.345 align:middle line:90%
I mean, they're supposed to be--

00:28:04.345 --> 00:28:06.220 align:middle line:84%
I mean, we know they're
not perfect integers,

00:28:06.220 --> 00:28:07.400 align:middle line:90%
but this is way off.

00:28:07.400 --> 00:28:10.780 align:middle line:84%
And the reason is because
what's actually measured

00:28:10.780 --> 00:28:12.278 align:middle line:90%
is the mass over charge.

00:28:12.278 --> 00:28:14.320 align:middle line:84%
You're not measuring the
mass-- mass over charge.

00:28:14.320 --> 00:28:17.470 align:middle line:84%
And so this is saying this
particular peptide has a +2

00:28:17.470 --> 00:28:18.190 align:middle line:90%
charge state.

00:28:18.190 --> 00:28:19.910 align:middle line:90%
That's an important fact.

00:28:19.910 --> 00:28:22.290 align:middle line:84%
It's going to be hard
to interpret its mass

00:28:22.290 --> 00:28:24.040 align:middle line:84%
if you don't know its
charge, because it's

00:28:24.040 --> 00:28:27.063 align:middle line:90%
m/z that's measured.

00:28:27.063 --> 00:28:28.480 align:middle line:84%
The other thing
that's measured is

00:28:28.480 --> 00:28:30.503 align:middle line:84%
from the exact
binomial distribution

00:28:30.503 --> 00:28:32.920 align:middle line:84%
you can get an estimate of the
number of carbons in there,

00:28:32.920 --> 00:28:35.290 align:middle line:84%
because if they're a
huge number of carbons,

00:28:35.290 --> 00:28:39.430 align:middle line:84%
then it will turn out that one
of the secondary peaks here,

00:28:39.430 --> 00:28:43.000 align:middle line:84%
one of the rightward
peaks, will actually

00:28:43.000 --> 00:28:44.140 align:middle line:90%
be the most abundant one.

00:28:44.140 --> 00:28:45.760 align:middle line:84%
If it has a small
number of carbons,

00:28:45.760 --> 00:28:50.200 align:middle line:84%
then the zero carbon-13 peak
will be the all around winner.

00:28:50.200 --> 00:28:54.135 align:middle line:84%
And so from the relative
heights of 0, 1, and 2,

00:28:54.135 --> 00:28:55.760 align:middle line:84%
you can estimate the
number of carbons.

00:28:55.760 --> 00:28:58.690 align:middle line:84%
So two facts you can get
from this high resolution

00:28:58.690 --> 00:29:02.650 align:middle line:90%
view of that peak.

00:29:02.650 --> 00:29:11.440 align:middle line:84%
So now we've got these two
phases, these two dimensions

00:29:11.440 --> 00:29:13.270 align:middle line:90%
pretty well in hand--

00:29:13.270 --> 00:29:14.860 align:middle line:90%
the reverse phase and the mass.

00:29:14.860 --> 00:29:16.750 align:middle line:84%
Now we're going to add
another dimension--

00:29:16.750 --> 00:29:18.260 align:middle line:84%
actually, a couple
more dimensions.

00:29:18.260 --> 00:29:19.660 align:middle line:84%
One is another
peptide dimension,

00:29:19.660 --> 00:29:24.280 align:middle line:84%
which is a Strong
Cation Exchange, SCX.

00:29:24.280 --> 00:29:26.170 align:middle line:84%
What this means is
the peptides will

00:29:26.170 --> 00:29:28.505 align:middle line:84%
have different cationic
properties, different charges,

00:29:28.505 --> 00:29:31.060 align:middle line:84%
and they will bind to
different extents to this.

00:29:31.060 --> 00:29:34.990 align:middle line:84%
And you can put these in
tandem, either physically

00:29:34.990 --> 00:29:36.100 align:middle line:90%
or conceptually.

00:29:36.100 --> 00:29:38.008 align:middle line:84%
You'll run one, take
a bunch of fractions,

00:29:38.008 --> 00:29:39.550 align:middle line:84%
and then put it on
the reverse phase.

00:29:39.550 --> 00:29:42.460 align:middle line:84%
The first phase literally
is physically connected

00:29:42.460 --> 00:29:45.040 align:middle line:90%
to the mass spectrometer.

00:29:45.040 --> 00:29:47.690 align:middle line:84%
And then we'll talk
about some upstream.

00:29:47.690 --> 00:29:50.290 align:middle line:84%
Now, upstream
separation methods,

00:29:50.290 --> 00:29:53.400 align:middle line:84%
before you fragment it into
peptides, you can separate.

00:29:53.400 --> 00:29:56.170 align:middle line:84%
It can have dimensions on the
proteins, which can tell you

00:29:56.170 --> 00:29:58.120 align:middle line:90%
about the complexes.

00:29:58.120 --> 00:30:01.310 align:middle line:84%
Let's go through a specific
example of complexes.

00:30:01.310 --> 00:30:03.860 align:middle line:90%


00:30:03.860 --> 00:30:07.000 align:middle line:84%
So if you take the
entire yeast proteome--

00:30:07.000 --> 00:30:12.940 align:middle line:84%
grind up yeast, throw it
on a first separation,

00:30:12.940 --> 00:30:18.400 align:middle line:84%
which is the sedimentation
velocity-- remember,

00:30:18.400 --> 00:30:20.050 align:middle line:84%
there's equilibrium
and velocity.

00:30:20.050 --> 00:30:24.055 align:middle line:84%
Velocity is mainly responsive
to the size of the complex.

00:30:24.055 --> 00:30:25.180 align:middle line:90%
This is a native dimension.

00:30:25.180 --> 00:30:27.820 align:middle line:84%
We're not denaturing
with SDS native.

00:30:27.820 --> 00:30:29.380 align:middle line:84%
And so you'll see
the things that

00:30:29.380 --> 00:30:32.260 align:middle line:84%
go absolutely the
fastest in the cell

00:30:32.260 --> 00:30:36.640 align:middle line:84%
and among the most
abundant are the ribosomes

00:30:36.640 --> 00:30:38.020 align:middle line:90%
and the ribosomal subunits.

00:30:38.020 --> 00:30:40.180 align:middle line:84%
This is done under
conditions where you just

00:30:40.180 --> 00:30:44.950 align:middle line:84%
tease apart the two ribosomal
subunits so that they're

00:30:44.950 --> 00:30:45.670 align:middle line:90%
separated.

00:30:45.670 --> 00:30:48.490 align:middle line:84%
And the bigger of the
two is called 60S.

00:30:48.490 --> 00:30:50.980 align:middle line:84%
The S refers to
Svedberg, who was

00:30:50.980 --> 00:30:55.870 align:middle line:84%
one of the pioneers of
sedimentation velocity.

00:30:55.870 --> 00:30:59.890 align:middle line:84%
And this can be correlated.
the Rate at which it goes down,

00:30:59.890 --> 00:31:04.690 align:middle line:84%
this stabilized gradient
centrifugal field is

00:31:04.690 --> 00:31:07.030 align:middle line:90%
related to the complex.

00:31:07.030 --> 00:31:09.040 align:middle line:84%
And then so that's
the first dimension

00:31:09.040 --> 00:31:10.990 align:middle line:90%
is horizontally, sedimentation.

00:31:10.990 --> 00:31:15.940 align:middle line:84%
The very top axis going
horizontally is sedimentation.

00:31:15.940 --> 00:31:22.030 align:middle line:84%
And then going down is
SDS gel electrophoresis.

00:31:22.030 --> 00:31:24.070 align:middle line:84%
So now you're taking
these native complexes

00:31:24.070 --> 00:31:27.190 align:middle line:84%
and breaking them up into
their component proteins,

00:31:27.190 --> 00:31:29.020 align:middle line:84%
where high molecular
weight is up,

00:31:29.020 --> 00:31:30.520 align:middle line:84%
and low molecular
weight is down.

00:31:30.520 --> 00:31:32.895 align:middle line:84%
And you can see that there
are quite a number of proteins

00:31:32.895 --> 00:31:35.320 align:middle line:84%
in each of these two
subunits, the 60S and the 40S.

00:31:35.320 --> 00:31:38.080 align:middle line:84%
Now, each of these proteins--
so that's the second dimension.

00:31:38.080 --> 00:31:40.780 align:middle line:84%
Now, you cleave
it into peptides,

00:31:40.780 --> 00:31:45.250 align:middle line:84%
and you analyze the peptides by
retention time in all the three

00:31:45.250 --> 00:31:46.690 align:middle line:90%
dimensions--

00:31:46.690 --> 00:31:49.210 align:middle line:84%
strong cation exchange,
retention time, and mass.

00:31:49.210 --> 00:31:50.710 align:middle line:84%
And then you add a
fourth one, which

00:31:50.710 --> 00:31:53.230 align:middle line:84%
we'll develop a little
later, where you can actually

00:31:53.230 --> 00:31:55.280 align:middle line:84%
break up the peptides
into little pieces.

00:31:55.280 --> 00:32:00.560 align:middle line:84%
So you identify which protein
each of the peptides came from.

00:32:00.560 --> 00:32:03.580 align:middle line:84%
Now, unfortunately,
not all peptides

00:32:03.580 --> 00:32:06.440 align:middle line:84%
are equal in their
ionization potential.

00:32:06.440 --> 00:32:09.160 align:middle line:84%
And so if it ionizes
poorly, you won't

00:32:09.160 --> 00:32:12.310 align:middle line:84%
detect a particular
peptide from a protein.

00:32:12.310 --> 00:32:14.710 align:middle line:84%
If you detect a large number
of peptides from a protein,

00:32:14.710 --> 00:32:17.050 align:middle line:84%
that probably means that
it's abundant in your sample

00:32:17.050 --> 00:32:19.630 align:middle line:84%
or your fraction,
and it also means

00:32:19.630 --> 00:32:21.880 align:middle line:84%
that you can believe
that the computer

00:32:21.880 --> 00:32:25.520 align:middle line:84%
identification of that protein
is probably pretty solid.

00:32:25.520 --> 00:32:28.210 align:middle line:84%
So if you get five or more
peptides from a particular

00:32:28.210 --> 00:32:30.402 align:middle line:84%
protein in your
database search--

00:32:30.402 --> 00:32:32.860 align:middle line:84%
and we'll talk just a moment
about how you do that database

00:32:32.860 --> 00:32:34.060 align:middle line:90%
search--

00:32:34.060 --> 00:32:36.130 align:middle line:84%
then you believe it,
and it's very solid.

00:32:36.130 --> 00:32:39.160 align:middle line:84%
And so if you look at the-- you
can see that all these-- as you

00:32:39.160 --> 00:32:44.260 align:middle line:84%
look at essentially the protein
fingerprint for each of the 60S

00:32:44.260 --> 00:32:47.122 align:middle line:84%
fractions in the sedimentation,
they look pretty similar.

00:32:47.122 --> 00:32:48.580 align:middle line:84%
And when you run
out the mass spec,

00:32:48.580 --> 00:32:53.110 align:middle line:84%
you get similar sets
of peptide signatures.

00:32:53.110 --> 00:32:55.990 align:middle line:90%
And they correspond to--

00:32:55.990 --> 00:32:58.540 align:middle line:84%
most of them, especially
the most abundant ones,

00:32:58.540 --> 00:33:02.410 align:middle line:84%
correspond to the
known 60S proteins.

00:33:02.410 --> 00:33:07.530 align:middle line:84%
If you go a little
bit slower, less mass,

00:33:07.530 --> 00:33:11.190 align:middle line:84%
the 40S subunits and you
analyze the subsequences

00:33:11.190 --> 00:33:14.640 align:middle line:84%
of the mass spec signatures
for those peptides,

00:33:14.640 --> 00:33:16.860 align:middle line:90%
then they mainly turn up 40S.

00:33:16.860 --> 00:33:18.570 align:middle line:90%
There's some exceptions in both.

00:33:18.570 --> 00:33:22.020 align:middle line:84%
There are some other categories,
which may be interesting.

00:33:22.020 --> 00:33:26.500 align:middle line:84%
The most interesting in
this particular study,

00:33:26.500 --> 00:33:32.130 align:middle line:84%
the authors highlighted was
[? Weimer ?] 116p, which,

00:33:32.130 --> 00:33:33.780 align:middle line:84%
remember, this is a
very mature field.

00:33:33.780 --> 00:33:35.340 align:middle line:90%
This is a very recent study.

00:33:35.340 --> 00:33:37.770 align:middle line:84%
And ribosomes were very
well characterized.

00:33:37.770 --> 00:33:39.900 align:middle line:84%
And I think we had the
conceit that at least

00:33:39.900 --> 00:33:42.040 align:middle line:84%
in microbial systems,
such as E. coli and yeast,

00:33:42.040 --> 00:33:44.700 align:middle line:84%
we really understood all the
proteins that were required

00:33:44.700 --> 00:33:46.980 align:middle line:90%
to make a ribosome hum.

00:33:46.980 --> 00:33:49.650 align:middle line:84%
But here was a new
ribosomal protein,

00:33:49.650 --> 00:33:56.460 align:middle line:84%
which has since been confirmed
that this is a bona fide part

00:33:56.460 --> 00:33:57.510 align:middle line:90%
of the 40S subunit.

00:33:57.510 --> 00:34:00.160 align:middle line:84%
You can see here it
had many peptide hits.

00:34:00.160 --> 00:34:05.580 align:middle line:84%
So it was equal in abundance to
the other 40S subunit proteins.

00:34:05.580 --> 00:34:10.489 align:middle line:90%


00:34:10.489 --> 00:34:12.889 align:middle line:84%
So here we had in
a certain sense

00:34:12.889 --> 00:34:16.010 align:middle line:84%
five or so dimensions,
the sedimentation,

00:34:16.010 --> 00:34:18.949 align:middle line:84%
which is the native complex
size, the denatured protein

00:34:18.949 --> 00:34:23.525 align:middle line:84%
subunit size, the
peptide ionic--

00:34:23.525 --> 00:34:29.100 align:middle line:90%


00:34:29.100 --> 00:34:33.270 align:middle line:84%
the ion exchange,
the peptide mass,

00:34:33.270 --> 00:34:36.480 align:middle line:90%
and the peptide fragmentation.

00:34:36.480 --> 00:34:38.400 align:middle line:84%
So when we talk
about fragmentation,

00:34:38.400 --> 00:34:40.320 align:middle line:84%
that's what MS/MS
sometimes means.

00:34:40.320 --> 00:34:43.530 align:middle line:84%
It means that you're doing first
mass separation of the peptide.

00:34:43.530 --> 00:34:45.870 align:middle line:84%
You break it up, and you
do another mass separation

00:34:45.870 --> 00:34:47.070 align:middle line:90%
of the component parts.

00:34:47.070 --> 00:34:48.810 align:middle line:84%
And that allows
us to do database

00:34:48.810 --> 00:34:53.170 align:middle line:84%
searching and sequencing
of the peptides.

00:34:53.170 --> 00:34:54.159 align:middle line:90%
Now, how this works--

00:34:54.159 --> 00:34:55.365 align:middle line:84%
this is a blow up
of something that

00:34:55.365 --> 00:34:57.840 align:middle line:84%
was in an earlier slide, where
you can really see the region

00:34:57.840 --> 00:34:58.800 align:middle line:90%
where you've got electrospray.

00:34:58.800 --> 00:35:01.420 align:middle line:84%
We'll have an even closer
blow up with this in a moment.

00:35:01.420 --> 00:35:05.340 align:middle line:84%
But basically, your reverse
phase liquid chromatography

00:35:05.340 --> 00:35:07.350 align:middle line:84%
is going directly
into this vacuum

00:35:07.350 --> 00:35:13.560 align:middle line:84%
here, generating little
spray at 4,000 volts,

00:35:13.560 --> 00:35:18.060 align:middle line:84%
and then these molecular ions
will go through the vacuum

00:35:18.060 --> 00:35:22.260 align:middle line:84%
through a series of
magnetic octopoles

00:35:22.260 --> 00:35:24.840 align:middle line:84%
until it hits an ion
trap, which helps

00:35:24.840 --> 00:35:29.258 align:middle line:84%
you determine the m/z,
and finally a detector.

00:35:29.258 --> 00:35:31.800 align:middle line:84%
You can see there's a variety
of different pressures in here,

00:35:31.800 --> 00:35:34.770 align:middle line:84%
sort of increasing pressure
from the point where you have

00:35:34.770 --> 00:35:37.410 align:middle line:84%
the aqueous solvent
going in all the way

00:35:37.410 --> 00:35:43.303 align:middle line:84%
to the detector, which is the
highest vacuum at around 10

00:35:43.303 --> 00:35:47.490 align:middle line:90%
to the minus fifth torr.

00:35:47.490 --> 00:35:49.710 align:middle line:84%
Now, here's where the
[INAUDIBLE] mass spectrometry,

00:35:49.710 --> 00:35:57.510 align:middle line:84%
or MS/MS, or collision-induced
dissociation, you have--

00:35:57.510 --> 00:36:00.045 align:middle line:84%
here's your ion beam of
molecular peptide ions.

00:36:00.045 --> 00:36:03.930 align:middle line:90%
You've now turned peptides in--

00:36:03.930 --> 00:36:06.900 align:middle line:84%
they're each on their
own little space.

00:36:06.900 --> 00:36:09.360 align:middle line:84%
And in the middle
of this quadrupole,

00:36:09.360 --> 00:36:13.800 align:middle line:84%
this magnetic
environment here, you

00:36:13.800 --> 00:36:17.760 align:middle line:84%
bring in an inert
gas, like argon,

00:36:17.760 --> 00:36:22.290 align:middle line:84%
to collide with these
rapidly moving ions,

00:36:22.290 --> 00:36:24.990 align:middle line:84%
and they will break
the chain, basically,

00:36:24.990 --> 00:36:26.370 align:middle line:90%
at any covalent bond.

00:36:26.370 --> 00:36:31.290 align:middle line:84%
And if you think of the peptide
at a chain backbone as having

00:36:31.290 --> 00:36:34.470 align:middle line:84%
three different covalent
bonds, there's the peptide bond

00:36:34.470 --> 00:36:37.500 align:middle line:84%
itself, and then there's
a carbon-nitrogen bond,

00:36:37.500 --> 00:36:41.610 align:middle line:90%
and a carbonyl nitrogen bond--

00:36:41.610 --> 00:36:43.650 align:middle line:84%
sorry-- and a
carbon-carbon bond.

00:36:43.650 --> 00:36:45.870 align:middle line:84%
It can break at any of
those three positions,

00:36:45.870 --> 00:36:49.260 align:middle line:84%
and then you'll generate a
set of fragments coming in

00:36:49.260 --> 00:36:54.390 align:middle line:84%
from the N-terminus with three
possible C-terminal fragments.

00:36:54.390 --> 00:36:57.815 align:middle line:84%
And coming in, if a C-terminus
is cleaving at the same point,

00:36:57.815 --> 00:36:59.440 align:middle line:84%
the whole series is
coming in that way.

00:36:59.440 --> 00:37:02.370 align:middle line:84%
And as you're coming
in from the N-terminus,

00:37:02.370 --> 00:37:04.800 align:middle line:84%
you give it A, B,
and C, depending

00:37:04.800 --> 00:37:07.620 align:middle line:84%
on whether it breaks
at the C carbon-carbon

00:37:07.620 --> 00:37:10.560 align:middle line:84%
bond, the carbon-nitrogen bond,
or the nitrogen-carbon bond, A,

00:37:10.560 --> 00:37:11.940 align:middle line:90%
B, and C respectively.

00:37:11.940 --> 00:37:14.820 align:middle line:84%
And the same ones coming
in from the C-terminus

00:37:14.820 --> 00:37:20.680 align:middle line:84%
are called Z, Y, and X.
And so, as it turns out,

00:37:20.680 --> 00:37:24.630 align:middle line:84%
just empirically, if you sort
through all the chemistry,

00:37:24.630 --> 00:37:30.240 align:middle line:84%
in most cases, the
peptide bond is the one

00:37:30.240 --> 00:37:32.640 align:middle line:90%
that's most actively cleaved.

00:37:32.640 --> 00:37:36.720 align:middle line:84%
And so the B ions and
their complementary Y ions

00:37:36.720 --> 00:37:37.960 align:middle line:90%
dominate the picture.

00:37:37.960 --> 00:37:39.460 align:middle line:84%
Now, the other ones
will be present.

00:37:39.460 --> 00:37:42.120 align:middle line:84%
Especially if they come from
a very abundant peptide,

00:37:42.120 --> 00:37:46.170 align:middle line:84%
they can swap out
the B and Y ions

00:37:46.170 --> 00:37:47.640 align:middle line:90%
for a less abundant peptide.

00:37:47.640 --> 00:37:50.400 align:middle line:84%
But all other things being
equal, B and Y will dominate,

00:37:50.400 --> 00:37:53.025 align:middle line:84%
and most of the rest of the
discussion will be about those.

00:37:53.025 --> 00:37:56.640 align:middle line:90%


00:37:56.640 --> 00:37:58.770 align:middle line:84%
This is the closest
picture we'll

00:37:58.770 --> 00:38:00.360 align:middle line:90%
show of the ionization step.

00:38:00.360 --> 00:38:06.480 align:middle line:84%
This is a step, which has not
been thoroughly enough studied,

00:38:06.480 --> 00:38:10.110 align:middle line:84%
in the sense that this
ionization step, where

00:38:10.110 --> 00:38:14.670 align:middle line:84%
the droplets of
aqueous and organic

00:38:14.670 --> 00:38:18.090 align:middle line:84%
solvent coming out of
the separation column

00:38:18.090 --> 00:38:21.210 align:middle line:90%
is subjected to the vacuum.

00:38:21.210 --> 00:38:24.240 align:middle line:84%
The water starts being
released from the droplet.

00:38:24.240 --> 00:38:27.420 align:middle line:84%
The protons-- remember,
this acidic media associates

00:38:27.420 --> 00:38:28.740 align:middle line:90%
with a molecular ion.

00:38:28.740 --> 00:38:30.630 align:middle line:84%
They kind of explode
because there's

00:38:30.630 --> 00:38:33.480 align:middle line:84%
too much positive
charge in a small space,

00:38:33.480 --> 00:38:36.660 align:middle line:84%
until you finally have a
molecular ion associated

00:38:36.660 --> 00:38:40.103 align:middle line:84%
with one or two net
positive charges.

00:38:40.103 --> 00:38:42.270 align:middle line:84%
Remember, we had an example
just a little while ago.

00:38:42.270 --> 00:38:43.728 align:middle line:84%
We had a net positive
charge of +2.

00:38:43.728 --> 00:38:46.560 align:middle line:90%


00:38:46.560 --> 00:38:49.860 align:middle line:84%
You don't need to have
neutrality in this situation.

00:38:49.860 --> 00:38:52.230 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, this is poorly
understood in the sense

00:38:52.230 --> 00:38:56.040 align:middle line:84%
that some peptides ionize
much better than others.

00:38:56.040 --> 00:38:58.710 align:middle line:84%
And we'll come back to this when
we talk about quantification.

00:38:58.710 --> 00:39:02.880 align:middle line:84%
But for right now, what we want
to do is ask how do we analyze

00:39:02.880 --> 00:39:05.910 align:middle line:84%
the complex spectra that
comes out when you fragment--

00:39:05.910 --> 00:39:07.440 align:middle line:90%
when you take--

00:39:07.440 --> 00:39:09.310 align:middle line:84%
first, you get a
fairly simple spectra,

00:39:09.310 --> 00:39:13.832 align:middle line:84%
which is just a list of all the
masses of all of the peptides.

00:39:13.832 --> 00:39:15.540 align:middle line:84%
And remember, some
will be weak, and some

00:39:15.540 --> 00:39:22.560 align:middle line:84%
will be strong because of
this voodoo ionization.

00:39:22.560 --> 00:39:23.808 align:middle line:90%
But then we break them.

00:39:23.808 --> 00:39:26.100 align:middle line:84%
However, whatever the intensity
of the original peptide

00:39:26.100 --> 00:39:28.170 align:middle line:84%
was, it will make a bunch
of daughter ions, which

00:39:28.170 --> 00:39:30.410 align:middle line:84%
will be the B ions coming
in from the N-terminus,

00:39:30.410 --> 00:39:32.170 align:middle line:90%
the Y ions from the C-terminus.

00:39:32.170 --> 00:39:34.800 align:middle line:84%
And you'll have this big
mixture, a nested set,

00:39:34.800 --> 00:39:37.895 align:middle line:84%
that get increasingly
large as you get further

00:39:37.895 --> 00:39:39.270 align:middle line:84%
from the N-terminus
and the B ion

00:39:39.270 --> 00:39:41.460 align:middle line:90%
series and their complements.

00:39:41.460 --> 00:39:44.640 align:middle line:84%
And the sum of the B ion
with its complementary Y ion

00:39:44.640 --> 00:39:47.880 align:middle line:84%
has to be the original
molecular mass corrected

00:39:47.880 --> 00:39:51.670 align:middle line:84%
for the chemistry that
occurs right at the cleavage.

00:39:51.670 --> 00:39:53.380 align:middle line:90%
And so here's a real example.

00:39:53.380 --> 00:39:55.740 align:middle line:84%
We're going to work
through it so that you

00:39:55.740 --> 00:39:59.460 align:middle line:84%
can see what happens
to a typical peptide

00:39:59.460 --> 00:40:02.470 align:middle line:84%
here in the upper
right-hand corner.

00:40:02.470 --> 00:40:05.300 align:middle line:84%
And this is tandem
mass spectrometry.

00:40:05.300 --> 00:40:07.050 align:middle line:84%
Remember, there is a--
if you think of it,

00:40:07.050 --> 00:40:13.860 align:middle line:84%
there was a single mass
peptide that was then broken

00:40:13.860 --> 00:40:15.450 align:middle line:90%
into all these little pieces.

00:40:15.450 --> 00:40:22.260 align:middle line:84%
And the almost
intact peptide will

00:40:22.260 --> 00:40:26.400 align:middle line:84%
be on the far end of the
horizontal axis, which

00:40:26.400 --> 00:40:30.760 align:middle line:84%
is the mass axis, close to
1,200 atomic mass units.

00:40:30.760 --> 00:40:36.150 align:middle line:84%
And then a relative abundance
is the vertical axis,

00:40:36.150 --> 00:40:39.870 align:middle line:84%
as it's just related
to the ion counts.

00:40:39.870 --> 00:40:43.200 align:middle line:84%
And you can see there's
some variation here.

00:40:43.200 --> 00:40:45.130 align:middle line:90%
This is not due to ionization.

00:40:45.130 --> 00:40:47.910 align:middle line:84%
This is due to the cleavage
efficiency, cleaving

00:40:47.910 --> 00:40:52.560 align:middle line:84%
at each of the bonds for the Y
series, which is in blue here.

00:40:52.560 --> 00:40:55.230 align:middle line:84%
And it tends to be the
higher peaks, and the B

00:40:55.230 --> 00:40:57.840 align:middle line:84%
series in red, which tends
to be slightly lower peaks.

00:40:57.840 --> 00:40:59.760 align:middle line:84%
And then you've got
these little arrows,

00:40:59.760 --> 00:41:02.700 align:middle line:84%
the darker arrows indicate
the Y ion series that

00:41:02.700 --> 00:41:04.500 align:middle line:90%
separate two adjacent peaks.

00:41:04.500 --> 00:41:07.080 align:middle line:84%
Because what's the difference
between those two peaks

00:41:07.080 --> 00:41:09.610 align:middle line:84%
is the addition
of one amino acid.

00:41:09.610 --> 00:41:15.012 align:middle line:84%
And so the focusing on
the blue series, the Y

00:41:15.012 --> 00:41:16.470 align:middle line:84%
ions coming in from
the C-terminus,

00:41:16.470 --> 00:41:20.280 align:middle line:84%
the shortest Y, the Y1, would be
just the C-terminal amino acid

00:41:20.280 --> 00:41:22.230 align:middle line:90%
itself, which would be arginine.

00:41:22.230 --> 00:41:23.910 align:middle line:84%
And its distance
from the origin would

00:41:23.910 --> 00:41:28.020 align:middle line:84%
be about the mass of
the arginine itself.

00:41:28.020 --> 00:41:29.620 align:middle line:84%
And then you add
a glycine to it,

00:41:29.620 --> 00:41:32.500 align:middle line:84%
which is a small delta,
and then an alanine,

00:41:32.500 --> 00:41:35.250 align:middle line:84%
and an isoleucine, and
serine, and so forth.

00:41:35.250 --> 00:41:38.940 align:middle line:84%
And you can see
here very clearly

00:41:38.940 --> 00:41:43.920 align:middle line:84%
the leucine and the asparagine
and the valine, so that Y ion

00:41:43.920 --> 00:41:45.660 align:middle line:90%
series all the way down.

00:41:45.660 --> 00:41:47.460 align:middle line:84%
The G is the last
one documented.

00:41:47.460 --> 00:41:50.730 align:middle line:84%
The N and the S at the
highest molecular weight

00:41:50.730 --> 00:41:52.140 align:middle line:90%
are not visible.

00:41:52.140 --> 00:41:53.940 align:middle line:84%
And actually, many
of these things

00:41:53.940 --> 00:41:56.070 align:middle line:90%
you'll have very weak peaks.

00:41:56.070 --> 00:41:58.740 align:middle line:84%
You'll essentially
have missing peaks

00:41:58.740 --> 00:42:02.560 align:middle line:84%
that are corresponding to
one of the delta amino acids.

00:42:02.560 --> 00:42:05.190 align:middle line:84%
So in that case, the distance
between the two prominent peaks

00:42:05.190 --> 00:42:06.710 align:middle line:90%
will be two amino acids.

00:42:06.710 --> 00:42:09.210 align:middle line:84%
You can see this starting to
get to be a challenging pattern

00:42:09.210 --> 00:42:11.760 align:middle line:84%
recognition problem, because
you've got all the B ions mixed

00:42:11.760 --> 00:42:12.602 align:middle line:90%
in with the Y ions.

00:42:12.602 --> 00:42:14.310 align:middle line:84%
And this is summarized
in the next slide.

00:42:14.310 --> 00:42:17.190 align:middle line:90%


00:42:17.190 --> 00:42:20.680 align:middle line:84%
The B ions are mixed
in with the Y ions.

00:42:20.680 --> 00:42:23.580 align:middle line:84%
And some of the
ions are missing.

00:42:23.580 --> 00:42:26.145 align:middle line:84%
Each ion has multiple
isotopic forms.

00:42:26.145 --> 00:42:28.020 align:middle line:84%
That wasn't so evident
in the previous slide.

00:42:28.020 --> 00:42:30.660 align:middle line:84%
But that blow up I
showed a while back,

00:42:30.660 --> 00:42:33.810 align:middle line:84%
you had that binomial
distribution.

00:42:33.810 --> 00:42:41.040 align:middle line:84%
There is the lingering
presence of A, C, X,

00:42:41.040 --> 00:42:44.070 align:middle line:84%
and Z type ions, where
you've got cleavage

00:42:44.070 --> 00:42:45.990 align:middle line:90%
of some of the other bonds.

00:42:45.990 --> 00:42:48.580 align:middle line:84%
Ions can lose a
water or an ammonia.

00:42:48.580 --> 00:42:50.820 align:middle line:84%
You've got noise
from other peptides

00:42:50.820 --> 00:42:53.370 align:middle line:84%
and from contaminants
in the system.

00:42:53.370 --> 00:42:56.730 align:middle line:84%
And you've got amino acid
modifications, which is not

00:42:56.730 --> 00:42:58.090 align:middle line:90%
a contaminant or a bad thing.

00:42:58.090 --> 00:42:58.530 align:middle line:90%
It's a good thing.

00:42:58.530 --> 00:42:59.863 align:middle line:90%
This is what you're looking for.

00:42:59.863 --> 00:43:02.370 align:middle line:84%
But these can be in trace
amounts in this system.

00:43:02.370 --> 00:43:07.990 align:middle line:90%


00:43:07.990 --> 00:43:10.060 align:middle line:84%
Now, there are two
ways to approach

00:43:10.060 --> 00:43:14.862 align:middle line:84%
the awesome amount of data
that you can get out of these.

00:43:14.862 --> 00:43:16.820 align:middle line:84%
Remember, you've got all
these multi dimensions

00:43:16.820 --> 00:43:21.460 align:middle line:84%
finally ending in this
forest of B ions and Y ions

00:43:21.460 --> 00:43:23.382 align:middle line:90%
and all the rest of it in there.

00:43:23.382 --> 00:43:24.590 align:middle line:90%
And there are two approaches.

00:43:24.590 --> 00:43:28.450 align:middle line:84%
One is we'll call it de novo
peptide sequencing, which

00:43:28.450 --> 00:43:32.200 align:middle line:84%
would be analogous to the
de novo DNA sequencing

00:43:32.200 --> 00:43:33.010 align:middle line:90%
that we were doing

00:43:33.010 --> 00:43:36.940 align:middle line:84%
and the other is if you
tell me the sequence then

00:43:36.940 --> 00:43:41.710 align:middle line:84%
I'll find it in my data kind of
game OK it's doing a database

00:43:41.710 --> 00:43:45.130 align:middle line:84%
search where you're limited to
proteins that are very, very--

00:43:45.130 --> 00:43:46.870 align:middle line:84%
to finding peptides
that you already

00:43:46.870 --> 00:43:50.870 align:middle line:84%
know about, or can hypothesize
from a genome sequence.

00:43:50.870 --> 00:43:52.180 align:middle line:90%
So this is the first category.

00:43:52.180 --> 00:43:53.770 align:middle line:90%
This is de novo sequencing.

00:43:53.770 --> 00:43:55.660 align:middle line:84%
And it takes on
all the challenges

00:43:55.660 --> 00:43:56.950 align:middle line:90%
in the previous slide.

00:43:56.950 --> 00:44:00.160 align:middle line:84%
It takes on the
possibility of missing data

00:44:00.160 --> 00:44:02.800 align:middle line:84%
for a particular ion species
that you think should be there,

00:44:02.800 --> 00:44:06.520 align:middle line:84%
but for some reason are
not efficiently cleaved

00:44:06.520 --> 00:44:10.540 align:middle line:84%
by the argon in the
collision-induced association.

00:44:10.540 --> 00:44:11.980 align:middle line:84%
And it takes into
account that you

00:44:11.980 --> 00:44:15.790 align:middle line:84%
have to have one set of B
ions, a nested set of masses

00:44:15.790 --> 00:44:18.070 align:middle line:84%
from the N-terminus
that have to be

00:44:18.070 --> 00:44:19.840 align:middle line:84%
complementary to
this nested set you

00:44:19.840 --> 00:44:21.590 align:middle line:90%
get coming in from C-terminus.

00:44:21.590 --> 00:44:23.475 align:middle line:90%
So this is dynamic programming.

00:44:23.475 --> 00:44:25.600 align:middle line:84%
And you can probably count
how many different times

00:44:25.600 --> 00:44:28.550 align:middle line:84%
we've done a dynamic programming
algorithm in this class.

00:44:28.550 --> 00:44:31.210 align:middle line:84%
And so hopefully you're happy
that you did at least one

00:44:31.210 --> 00:44:32.260 align:middle line:90%
of them by hand.

00:44:32.260 --> 00:44:36.460 align:middle line:84%
And this one we won't
belabor, but here you

00:44:36.460 --> 00:44:38.410 align:middle line:84%
can see how it kind
of conceptually

00:44:38.410 --> 00:44:40.475 align:middle line:84%
maps to the simplest
one that we talked

00:44:40.475 --> 00:44:42.850 align:middle line:84%
about at the beginning, which
is comparing two amino acid

00:44:42.850 --> 00:44:43.570 align:middle line:90%
sequences.

00:44:43.570 --> 00:44:47.470 align:middle line:84%
There, the indels were caused
by evolutionary change.

00:44:47.470 --> 00:44:49.570 align:middle line:84%
Here the insertions
and deletions

00:44:49.570 --> 00:44:53.290 align:middle line:84%
are due to a missing ion due to
inefficient cleavage in the gas

00:44:53.290 --> 00:44:55.540 align:middle line:90%
phase.

00:44:55.540 --> 00:44:58.330 align:middle line:84%
This is further complicated
by this necessity

00:44:58.330 --> 00:45:01.240 align:middle line:84%
of essentially sequencing
in both from the B and the Y

00:45:01.240 --> 00:45:03.250 align:middle line:84%
simultaneously and
making sure that you

00:45:03.250 --> 00:45:08.890 align:middle line:84%
have the best combination
of B and Y assignments.

00:45:08.890 --> 00:45:10.330 align:middle line:90%
So that's de novo sequencing.

00:45:10.330 --> 00:45:14.450 align:middle line:84%
Now, in slide 29, we
have the alternative,

00:45:14.450 --> 00:45:18.220 align:middle line:84%
which is by far more
commonly applied,

00:45:18.220 --> 00:45:20.920 align:middle line:84%
an example of the
alternative, which

00:45:20.920 --> 00:45:22.700 align:middle line:90%
is you tell me a sequence.

00:45:22.700 --> 00:45:25.300 align:middle line:84%
I'll find it in my
data, [INAUDIBLE]..

00:45:25.300 --> 00:45:28.330 align:middle line:84%
And where you're basically
calculating the spectrum

00:45:28.330 --> 00:45:30.610 align:middle line:84%
that you might expect
for each peptide

00:45:30.610 --> 00:45:32.550 align:middle line:84%
that you might
expect in a genome.

00:45:32.550 --> 00:45:34.300 align:middle line:84%
So you basically use
the genome to predict

00:45:34.300 --> 00:45:35.920 align:middle line:90%
the protein coding regions.

00:45:35.920 --> 00:45:39.430 align:middle line:84%
Use those to do in
silico digestion

00:45:39.430 --> 00:45:41.710 align:middle line:84%
with trypsin,
basically cleave after

00:45:41.710 --> 00:45:46.250 align:middle line:84%
every lysine and arginine, and
maybe after some other ones

00:45:46.250 --> 00:45:49.180 align:middle line:84%
are complicated rules where
trypsin doesn't always cleave

00:45:49.180 --> 00:45:50.467 align:middle line:90%
after lysines and arginines.

00:45:50.467 --> 00:45:52.550 align:middle line:84%
And sometimes there'll be
other proteases present.

00:45:52.550 --> 00:45:54.217 align:middle line:84%
And you have to take
those into account.

00:45:54.217 --> 00:45:56.800 align:middle line:84%
In any case, you generate
a virtual set of peptides.

00:45:56.800 --> 00:45:59.500 align:middle line:84%
You generate a virtual
set of mass peaks.

00:45:59.500 --> 00:46:02.170 align:middle line:84%
Now, since we don't
know the rules that

00:46:02.170 --> 00:46:04.240 align:middle line:84%
determine the height
of those mass peaks,

00:46:04.240 --> 00:46:07.960 align:middle line:84%
we wish we did, but we don't,
so we just set it to a unit,

00:46:07.960 --> 00:46:10.580 align:middle line:84%
to some arbitrary to
make them all the same.

00:46:10.580 --> 00:46:13.300 align:middle line:84%
So you're not going to be
getting a great correlation

00:46:13.300 --> 00:46:15.220 align:middle line:84%
coefficient based
on the heights,

00:46:15.220 --> 00:46:18.080 align:middle line:84%
but merely whether
it's there or not.

00:46:18.080 --> 00:46:20.530 align:middle line:84%
And that's what you do
is every time you have

00:46:20.530 --> 00:46:25.525 align:middle line:84%
a hit between your predicted
spectrum and the other one,

00:46:25.525 --> 00:46:27.400 align:middle line:84%
no matter what the
intensity of the other one

00:46:27.400 --> 00:46:30.190 align:middle line:84%
is, so you waited on
the observed intensity,

00:46:30.190 --> 00:46:33.880 align:middle line:84%
but you have no real
calculated intensity.

00:46:33.880 --> 00:46:37.120 align:middle line:84%
And this correlation
coefficient serves as a way

00:46:37.120 --> 00:46:39.610 align:middle line:90%
of prioritizing your scores.

00:46:39.610 --> 00:46:41.230 align:middle line:90%
And if you have a--

00:46:41.230 --> 00:46:47.380 align:middle line:84%
very often the best score
will be the database hit

00:46:47.380 --> 00:46:49.030 align:middle line:90%
for the peptide that you want.

00:46:49.030 --> 00:46:53.320 align:middle line:84%
Now, if you're expecting
post-synthetic modifications,

00:46:53.320 --> 00:46:56.540 align:middle line:84%
you need to tell the algorithm
to add the appropriate mass

00:46:56.540 --> 00:46:57.920 align:middle line:90%
to the appropriate amino acid.

00:46:57.920 --> 00:47:00.580 align:middle line:84%
So for example, if you
expect a phosphoserine,

00:47:00.580 --> 00:47:03.610 align:middle line:84%
you have to put the phosphate
mass into the program

00:47:03.610 --> 00:47:05.120 align:middle line:90%
and associate it with a serine.

00:47:05.120 --> 00:47:07.330 align:middle line:84%
So the serine can be
either a regular serine

00:47:07.330 --> 00:47:08.140 align:middle line:90%
or a phosphoserine.

00:47:08.140 --> 00:47:12.100 align:middle line:84%
So that's another
complexity there.

00:47:12.100 --> 00:47:16.030 align:middle line:84%
So now we've gone through the
richness of the separation

00:47:16.030 --> 00:47:16.720 align:middle line:90%
methods.

00:47:16.720 --> 00:47:19.090 align:middle line:84%
Separation is
intimately connected

00:47:19.090 --> 00:47:22.120 align:middle line:84%
with getting us to a mass
spectrum which is clean enough

00:47:22.120 --> 00:47:25.150 align:middle line:84%
to do either de novo sequencing
or database searching.

00:47:25.150 --> 00:47:27.130 align:middle line:84%
Now that we've
got it identified,

00:47:27.130 --> 00:47:28.840 align:middle line:90%
let's try to quantitate it.

00:47:28.840 --> 00:47:31.840 align:middle line:84%
We can quantitate it one
of two ways, just the same

00:47:31.840 --> 00:47:34.135 align:middle line:84%
as with the RNAs, either
on an absolute scale

00:47:34.135 --> 00:47:36.970 align:middle line:90%
or on a relative scale.

00:47:36.970 --> 00:47:38.005 align:middle line:90%
What is involved?

00:47:38.005 --> 00:47:40.660 align:middle line:90%


00:47:40.660 --> 00:47:45.640 align:middle line:84%
We'll make an analogy to the
RNA quantification methods,

00:47:45.640 --> 00:47:47.590 align:middle line:84%
which I believe
we've had something

00:47:47.590 --> 00:47:50.200 align:middle line:84%
very similar to the
left-hand side of slide 31

00:47:50.200 --> 00:47:52.253 align:middle line:84%
here, when we talked about
RNAs, all the ways we

00:47:52.253 --> 00:47:53.170 align:middle line:90%
could quantitate them.

00:47:53.170 --> 00:47:58.060 align:middle line:84%
A subset of these have
an analog in the protein

00:47:58.060 --> 00:48:01.190 align:middle line:84%
domain on the right-hand
side of this slide.

00:48:01.190 --> 00:48:05.260 align:middle line:84%
So for example, one of our
favorite methods that we used

00:48:05.260 --> 00:48:06.370 align:middle line:90%
was microarrays.

00:48:06.370 --> 00:48:08.690 align:middle line:90%
That's the top line of the RNA.

00:48:08.690 --> 00:48:10.580 align:middle line:84%
This is where you
would have the gene

00:48:10.580 --> 00:48:12.260 align:middle line:84%
segments, either
oligonucleotides

00:48:12.260 --> 00:48:15.050 align:middle line:84%
or [INAUDIBLE],, immobilized
on a microarray.

00:48:15.050 --> 00:48:19.370 align:middle line:84%
Fluorescently label
your RNA and quantitate.

00:48:19.370 --> 00:48:22.430 align:middle line:84%
For proteins, the
equivalent would

00:48:22.430 --> 00:48:26.420 align:middle line:84%
be an antibody array
aimed at unique features

00:48:26.420 --> 00:48:27.740 align:middle line:90%
of each protein.

00:48:27.740 --> 00:48:29.570 align:middle line:84%
This is in very early
days, because we

00:48:29.570 --> 00:48:30.500 align:middle line:90%
are antibody limited.

00:48:30.500 --> 00:48:34.340 align:middle line:84%
We do not have antibodies
against every protein surface

00:48:34.340 --> 00:48:35.090 align:middle line:90%
epitope.

00:48:35.090 --> 00:48:37.050 align:middle line:84%
And they are not
specific enough.

00:48:37.050 --> 00:48:41.090 align:middle line:90%
There's a lot of crosstalk.

00:48:41.090 --> 00:48:45.650 align:middle line:84%
We mentioned in the second
line that microarrays could not

00:48:45.650 --> 00:48:49.400 align:middle line:84%
measure the composition
of alternative slices,

00:48:49.400 --> 00:48:51.180 align:middle line:84%
or the size of
the messenger RNA.

00:48:51.180 --> 00:48:53.030 align:middle line:84%
So that was best done
by a Northern blot,

00:48:53.030 --> 00:48:55.220 align:middle line:84%
which actually
measured the size,

00:48:55.220 --> 00:48:57.890 align:middle line:90%
but was not high throughput.

00:48:57.890 --> 00:49:00.500 align:middle line:84%
The equivalent for that for
proteins is called a Western.

00:49:00.500 --> 00:49:04.670 align:middle line:84%
These are all puns on
Ed Southern's name.

00:49:04.670 --> 00:49:08.210 align:middle line:84%
The Westerns will
allow you to measure

00:49:08.210 --> 00:49:11.210 align:middle line:84%
the size of native or
denaturing proteins,

00:49:11.210 --> 00:49:12.800 align:middle line:84%
and then detect them
with antibodies.

00:49:12.800 --> 00:49:14.540 align:middle line:90%
Again, antibody limited.

00:49:14.540 --> 00:49:18.080 align:middle line:84%
If we had a technology
breakthrough that would give us

00:49:18.080 --> 00:49:20.930 align:middle line:84%
all the antibodies we
need, just like we had,

00:49:20.930 --> 00:49:24.620 align:middle line:84%
it's easy to dial up any
nucleic acid you want just

00:49:24.620 --> 00:49:26.430 align:middle line:90%
by synthesis.

00:49:26.430 --> 00:49:29.180 align:middle line:90%


00:49:29.180 --> 00:49:32.750 align:middle line:84%
There's no real equivalent
to PCR for a protein world.

00:49:32.750 --> 00:49:35.000 align:middle line:84%
You can tag proteins
with nucleic acids

00:49:35.000 --> 00:49:36.770 align:middle line:90%
and do PCR on the nucleic acids.

00:49:36.770 --> 00:49:41.480 align:middle line:84%
But there's no real direct
amplication on proteins.

00:49:41.480 --> 00:49:44.090 align:middle line:84%
Reporter constructs basically
work the same for each.

00:49:44.090 --> 00:49:47.180 align:middle line:84%
You have something that is
highly specific because you

00:49:47.180 --> 00:49:52.100 align:middle line:84%
constructed it in
vivo, but it is

00:49:52.100 --> 00:50:00.020 align:middle line:84%
a sum of all the RNA and
protein expression steps that

00:50:00.020 --> 00:50:01.265 align:middle line:90%
give you the reporter.

00:50:01.265 --> 00:50:04.010 align:middle line:90%


00:50:04.010 --> 00:50:06.440 align:middle line:84%
Fluorescent in
situ hybridization

00:50:06.440 --> 00:50:08.030 align:middle line:84%
in the case of
RNA or fluorescent

00:50:08.030 --> 00:50:10.220 align:middle line:84%
in situ antibodies
in case of proteins

00:50:10.220 --> 00:50:14.300 align:middle line:84%
is a great way of correlating
quasi quantitative information

00:50:14.300 --> 00:50:18.350 align:middle line:84%
with a subcellular or
suborganismal localization.

00:50:18.350 --> 00:50:20.750 align:middle line:84%
Tag counting, there's nothing
equivalent for proteins,

00:50:20.750 --> 00:50:22.205 align:middle line:84%
and mass spectrometry
can be used

00:50:22.205 --> 00:50:23.330 align:middle line:90%
for differentiable display.

00:50:23.330 --> 00:50:27.440 align:middle line:90%


00:50:27.440 --> 00:50:32.090 align:middle line:84%
What are the sort of numbers
of molecules we have?

00:50:32.090 --> 00:50:35.990 align:middle line:84%
A ballpark when we're dealing
with quantitation-- slide 32,

00:50:35.990 --> 00:50:37.620 align:middle line:90%
it depends on the organism.

00:50:37.620 --> 00:50:40.610 align:middle line:84%
Some of the simplest ones,
like you find in yeast,

00:50:40.610 --> 00:50:42.890 align:middle line:84%
we mentioned the
messenger RNA molecules

00:50:42.890 --> 00:50:48.920 align:middle line:84%
might be less than one per cell,
just stochastic fluctuations.

00:50:48.920 --> 00:50:56.270 align:middle line:84%
And in a human cell, it's
probably a fairly good

00:50:56.270 --> 00:50:59.630 align:middle line:84%
approximation or assumption
that almost every nucleotide

00:50:59.630 --> 00:51:03.950 align:middle line:84%
in the human genome
can be transcribed.

00:51:03.950 --> 00:51:08.060 align:middle line:84%
Maybe there's some
leakiness where

00:51:08.060 --> 00:51:10.550 align:middle line:84%
on the order of 1 in
10 to the fourth cells

00:51:10.550 --> 00:51:14.115 align:middle line:84%
will have a little leakiness
at any particular nucleotide.

00:51:14.115 --> 00:51:15.740 align:middle line:84%
So that's kind of
the background level.

00:51:15.740 --> 00:51:17.330 align:middle line:84%
It's 10 to the minus
fourth per cell.

00:51:17.330 --> 00:51:19.670 align:middle line:84%
And it's really only
achievable detection

00:51:19.670 --> 00:51:22.490 align:middle line:90%
with reverse transcriptase PCR.

00:51:22.490 --> 00:51:27.275 align:middle line:84%
The entire transcriptome
of a human cell

00:51:27.275 --> 00:51:31.400 align:middle line:84%
is on the order of half a
million transcripts messenger

00:51:31.400 --> 00:51:32.360 align:middle line:90%
RNAs.

00:51:32.360 --> 00:51:35.690 align:middle line:84%
And so if any particular
messenger RNA got up to 10

00:51:35.690 --> 00:51:37.520 align:middle line:90%
to the fifth, it would dominate.

00:51:37.520 --> 00:51:40.160 align:middle line:84%
And this happens in some
cases, like reticulocytes.

00:51:40.160 --> 00:51:44.180 align:middle line:84%
Maybe 90% of the messenger
RNA might be globin.

00:51:44.180 --> 00:51:46.390 align:middle line:84%
Now, for proteins,
you'll typically

00:51:46.390 --> 00:51:47.390 align:middle line:90%
have bursts of proteins.

00:51:47.390 --> 00:51:48.515 align:middle line:90%
You have one messenger RNA.

00:51:48.515 --> 00:51:51.050 align:middle line:84%
You might get 10 to
1,000 proteins made,

00:51:51.050 --> 00:51:52.920 align:middle line:90%
depending on the organism.

00:51:52.920 --> 00:51:58.160 align:middle line:84%
And so you typically have a
corresponding amplification

00:51:58.160 --> 00:51:59.120 align:middle line:90%
in the last line.

00:51:59.120 --> 00:52:04.790 align:middle line:90%


00:52:04.790 --> 00:52:12.710 align:middle line:84%
Now, when people assess casually
whether a particular method is

00:52:12.710 --> 00:52:18.980 align:middle line:84%
quantitative or not, they
can be easily intimidated.

00:52:18.980 --> 00:52:21.140 align:middle line:90%
So they might say--

00:52:21.140 --> 00:52:25.670 align:middle line:84%
I've commented that
the ionization of--

00:52:25.670 --> 00:52:27.695 align:middle line:84%
so ESI stands for
Electrospray Ionization

00:52:27.695 --> 00:52:31.430 align:middle line:90%
in mass spectrometry.

00:52:31.430 --> 00:52:37.580 align:middle line:84%
If you take a protein and
cleave it with trypsin,

00:52:37.580 --> 00:52:41.900 align:middle line:84%
in principle, every
tryptic fragment,

00:52:41.900 --> 00:52:45.770 align:middle line:84%
since trypsin cleaves pretty
close to completely fairly

00:52:45.770 --> 00:52:49.550 align:middle line:84%
easily, every tryptic
fragment, every peptide

00:52:49.550 --> 00:52:51.140 align:middle line:90%
should be equimolar.

00:52:51.140 --> 00:52:55.610 align:middle line:84%
If you now inject that into
the HPLC, into the mass spec,

00:52:55.610 --> 00:53:00.740 align:middle line:84%
every peak integrated
intensity should be equal,

00:53:00.740 --> 00:53:02.090 align:middle line:90%
because they're all equimolar.

00:53:02.090 --> 00:53:08.660 align:middle line:84%
And then when you find that,
no, they vary over two orders

00:53:08.660 --> 00:53:12.410 align:middle line:84%
of magnitude-- that is to say
some are 100 times more intense

00:53:12.410 --> 00:53:13.738 align:middle line:90%
peaks than others--

00:53:13.738 --> 00:53:15.530 align:middle line:84%
then you might get
discouraged and say, oh,

00:53:15.530 --> 00:53:19.700 align:middle line:84%
isn't a quantitative
science at all.

00:53:19.700 --> 00:53:24.530 align:middle line:84%
I can't deal with a
factor of 100 difference.

00:53:24.530 --> 00:53:27.800 align:middle line:84%
But I think that you need
to reassess that when

00:53:27.800 --> 00:53:29.750 align:middle line:84%
people say that mass
spectrometry is not

00:53:29.750 --> 00:53:31.280 align:middle line:90%
quantitative.

00:53:31.280 --> 00:53:35.630 align:middle line:84%
The two requirements
for quantitation

00:53:35.630 --> 00:53:38.330 align:middle line:84%
is that you have
reproducibility,

00:53:38.330 --> 00:53:45.320 align:middle line:84%
and that you have a way of
calibrating or calculating.

00:53:45.320 --> 00:53:47.317 align:middle line:84%
If you can calculate
from first principles,

00:53:47.317 --> 00:53:48.650 align:middle line:90%
then you don't need calibration.

00:53:48.650 --> 00:53:51.480 align:middle line:84%
If it's too empirical,
then you need calibration.

00:53:51.480 --> 00:53:57.620 align:middle line:84%
But you do not need that
every disparate object behaves

00:53:57.620 --> 00:53:59.570 align:middle line:90%
exactly the same way.

00:53:59.570 --> 00:54:03.650 align:middle line:84%
Not every peptide has to give
the same quantitative answer

00:54:03.650 --> 00:54:06.980 align:middle line:84%
simply has to be reproducible
and calibrate able

00:54:06.980 --> 00:54:11.240 align:middle line:84%
with that same peptide and so
here's an example of establish

00:54:11.240 --> 00:54:13.580 align:middle line:84%
two examples in a
row of establishing

00:54:13.580 --> 00:54:19.880 align:middle line:84%
the reproducibility here this
is from that ribosomal protein

00:54:19.880 --> 00:54:23.570 align:middle line:84%
experiment that I showed
earlier with the complexes

00:54:23.570 --> 00:54:27.570 align:middle line:84%
of sedimentation velocity
and the multi dimensions.

00:54:27.570 --> 00:54:32.540 align:middle line:84%
And this is just you do
a measurement on day one,

00:54:32.540 --> 00:54:34.670 align:middle line:84%
and you do the whole
experiment over on day two,

00:54:34.670 --> 00:54:42.830 align:middle line:84%
and then you compare the
intensity of the peaks.

00:54:42.830 --> 00:54:46.430 align:middle line:84%
And you get what is a
fairly good straight line

00:54:46.430 --> 00:54:52.610 align:middle line:84%
relationship on a log-log
curve over about a little

00:54:52.610 --> 00:54:54.335 align:middle line:90%
over 3 logs.

00:54:54.335 --> 00:54:57.350 align:middle line:90%


00:54:57.350 --> 00:54:59.420 align:middle line:84%
There were many moving
parts in that experiment.

00:54:59.420 --> 00:55:01.520 align:middle line:84%
There were all those
different dimensions.

00:55:01.520 --> 00:55:04.280 align:middle line:84%
And the whole experiment was
not designed to be quantitative.

00:55:04.280 --> 00:55:07.130 align:middle line:84%
There are no internal controls,
and so forth, or so on.

00:55:07.130 --> 00:55:10.520 align:middle line:84%
Nevertheless, this is
a good starting point

00:55:10.520 --> 00:55:14.300 align:middle line:84%
for convincing yourself or
determining whether something

00:55:14.300 --> 00:55:17.840 align:middle line:84%
is reproducible enough that
you can make it quantitative.

00:55:17.840 --> 00:55:22.020 align:middle line:84%
Here's another way of
measuring the reproducibility.

00:55:22.020 --> 00:55:23.645 align:middle line:84%
That one was a
correlation coefficient,

00:55:23.645 --> 00:55:26.300 align:middle line:84%
a linear correlation
coefficient on a log-log plot.

00:55:26.300 --> 00:55:28.130 align:middle line:84%
Here is the coefficient
of variation.

00:55:28.130 --> 00:55:30.420 align:middle line:84%
I think we may have
mentioned this before.

00:55:30.420 --> 00:55:32.090 align:middle line:84%
This is just the
standard deviation

00:55:32.090 --> 00:55:33.650 align:middle line:90%
normalized by the mean.

00:55:33.650 --> 00:55:36.000 align:middle line:84%
In the upper left-hand
part of this slide,

00:55:36.000 --> 00:55:39.170 align:middle line:84%
you can see that the CV, or
Coefficient of Variation,

00:55:39.170 --> 00:55:41.490 align:middle line:84%
is just the standard
deviation divided by the mean.

00:55:41.490 --> 00:55:45.420 align:middle line:84%
So you can report it in terms
of percentages of variation.

00:55:45.420 --> 00:55:49.370 align:middle line:84%
So here with a calibration
standards of peptides,

00:55:49.370 --> 00:55:53.690 align:middle line:84%
you get somewhere between 2% and
28% coefficient of variation.

00:55:53.690 --> 00:55:57.860 align:middle line:84%
That means you can trust these
things to be within 2% to 28%

00:55:57.860 --> 00:56:02.400 align:middle line:84%
of their absolute amounts
when you calibrate them.

00:56:02.400 --> 00:56:06.350 align:middle line:84%
So these two are
just two examples

00:56:06.350 --> 00:56:09.080 align:middle line:84%
that should reassure you
that there is reproducibility

00:56:09.080 --> 00:56:10.760 align:middle line:90%
and you can calibrate.

00:56:10.760 --> 00:56:17.210 align:middle line:90%


00:56:17.210 --> 00:56:23.330 align:middle line:84%
Calibration can be an
expensive proposition.

00:56:23.330 --> 00:56:25.820 align:middle line:84%
But there are
various motivations

00:56:25.820 --> 00:56:30.200 align:middle line:84%
for measuring, quantitating
both proteins and nucleic acids

00:56:30.200 --> 00:56:31.130 align:middle line:90%
on an absolute scale.

00:56:31.130 --> 00:56:36.290 align:middle line:84%
And for example, you might want
to compare them to each other.

00:56:36.290 --> 00:56:40.430 align:middle line:84%
You might want to
say, to what extent

00:56:40.430 --> 00:56:43.580 align:middle line:84%
is it the case that the most
abundant proteins result

00:56:43.580 --> 00:56:45.710 align:middle line:84%
from the most abundant
messenger RNAs?

00:56:45.710 --> 00:56:48.560 align:middle line:84%
Or you could imagine
a world where

00:56:48.560 --> 00:56:51.140 align:middle line:84%
these are completely
independent, since one

00:56:51.140 --> 00:56:52.220 align:middle line:90%
is transcription factors.

00:56:52.220 --> 00:56:54.650 align:middle line:84%
The other is
translation factors.

00:56:54.650 --> 00:56:57.080 align:middle line:84%
There's no reason that they
necessarily are synced up.

00:56:57.080 --> 00:56:59.690 align:middle line:84%
Or you could
imagine a hypothesis

00:56:59.690 --> 00:57:02.150 align:middle line:84%
where it's a lot of work
to make a lot of protein,

00:57:02.150 --> 00:57:04.190 align:middle line:84%
and so everything has
to be working right

00:57:04.190 --> 00:57:05.840 align:middle line:90%
for the most abundant ones.

00:57:05.840 --> 00:57:07.340 align:middle line:84%
And for the least
abundant ones, you

00:57:07.340 --> 00:57:09.620 align:middle line:90%
can have a little more slop.

00:57:09.620 --> 00:57:14.340 align:middle line:84%
So this analysis
critiqued a little bit

00:57:14.340 --> 00:57:16.220 align:middle line:84%
in the subsequent
paper that we'll

00:57:16.220 --> 00:57:23.180 align:middle line:84%
talk about after the
break can be interpreted

00:57:23.180 --> 00:57:24.080 align:middle line:90%
as being consistent.

00:57:24.080 --> 00:57:25.400 align:middle line:84%
When you include
all the proteins,

00:57:25.400 --> 00:57:27.275 align:middle line:84%
you have a very good
correlation coefficient.

00:57:27.275 --> 00:57:29.870 align:middle line:84%
This is a linear Pearson
correlation coefficient.

00:57:29.870 --> 00:57:32.960 align:middle line:90%


00:57:32.960 --> 00:57:37.310 align:middle line:84%
But as you restrict yourself to
the lowest abundance proteins,

00:57:37.310 --> 00:57:38.840 align:middle line:90%
it falls apart.

00:57:38.840 --> 00:57:43.520 align:middle line:84%
You have less significant
Pearson correlation

00:57:43.520 --> 00:57:44.832 align:middle line:90%
coefficients.

00:57:44.832 --> 00:57:46.040 align:middle line:90%
So let's take a little break.

00:57:46.040 --> 00:57:49.440 align:middle line:84%
And we'll talk about
critiquing this a little bit,

00:57:49.440 --> 00:57:52.430 align:middle line:84%
and improving, and
asking other motivations

00:57:52.430 --> 00:57:56.950 align:middle line:84%
for putting protein on absolute
scale, and doing ratios.

00:57:56.950 --> 00:57:58.000 align:middle line:90%